"""A Level Geology (0755), Upper Sixth: Systematic Palaeontology, Stratigraphy & Regional Geology of Cameroon,
Economic, Petroleum, Hydro- & Engineering Geology, and the Examination module.

15 topic lessons and 3 examination lessons (slug prefix gyu2-), one practice set each (see the practice file of the
same name). Content follows KKEVO's master curriculum blueprint (module lines U6M3, U6M4 and U6M5) and the Board's
2023 Advanced Level subject report for exam structure and common errors; it is not a Board syllabus booklet.
Facts about the geology of Cameroon are limited to well-established points; ages, localities and unit names are
hedged "check your textbook" because textbooks and research papers differ. Teaching figures are labelled invented.
Calculations (radiometric ages, enrichment factors, oil window depths, porosity, Darcy flow, RQD, plasticity index,
factor of safety, coral growth lines) were checked by script.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("geology", "upper-sixth")

M_PAL = "Systematic Palaeontology"
M_STR = "Stratigraphy & Regional Geology of Cameroon"
M_ECO = "Economic, Petroleum, Hydro- & Engineering Geology"
M_EXAM = "Examination"

LESSONS = []

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Fossilization and taphonomy
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_PAL,
    topic="Fossilization and taphonomy",
    title="Taphonomy, modes of preservation and preservation potential",
    slug="gyu2-fossilization-taphonomy",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Taphonomy follows an organism from death to fossil: decay, disarticulation, transport, burial and diagenesis. It explains the modes of preservation, why some organisms have a high preservation potential, how fossils are classified and what they tell us about past environments.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the stages of taphonomy and the processes that destroy or preserve remains",
        "Distinguish the modes of preservation, from unaltered hard parts to moulds, casts and trace fossils",
        "Assess the preservation potential of a named organism from its skeleton, habitat and burial",
        "Use the Linnaean hierarchy and use fossils to reconstruct past environments",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Taphonomy", "Preservation potential", "Permineralization", "Replacement", "Mould and cast", "Trace fossil", "Lagerstätte", "Taxonomy"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Millions of animals live and die on a shallow sea floor, yet only a tiny fraction end up as fossils. At O Level you learnt that fossils form when hard parts are buried quickly. At A Level you must explain **why**: what happens to a body after death, which processes destroy it, which ones turn it into rock, and why a calcite shell has a better chance than a jellyfish. This lesson also gives the tools used in every later palaeontology lesson: the names of the ranks of classification and the way fossils are read as clues to past environments."),
        D("**Taphonomy** is the study of everything that happens to an organism from its death to its discovery as a fossil: decay, scavenging, disarticulation, transport, burial and diagenesis. **Preservation potential** is the likelihood that an organism, or a part of it, will be preserved as a fossil.", title="Taphonomy and preservation potential"),
        H("The taphonomic pathway"),
        KEY("From death to fossil", "Each stage removes information; only what survives every stage becomes a fossil.", cards=[
            ["1. Death and decay", "Soft tissues are eaten by scavengers and broken down by bacteria. Decay is fastest where there is oxygen; in anoxic (oxygen-free) water it is slow."],
            ["2. Disarticulation", "Once the soft tissue that holds a skeleton together has gone, the parts separate: the two valves of a bivalve, the segments of a trilobite, the plates of an echinoid."],
            ["3. Transport", "Currents move, sort, break and abrade the parts. Transported shells are often broken, worn, sorted by size or aligned by the current."],
            ["4. Burial", "Rapid burial by sediment protects remains from scavengers, oxygen and currents. It is the single most important condition."],
            ["5. Diagenesis", "After burial, compaction and chemical change in the sediment can dissolve, replace or fill the remains with new minerals."],
        ]),
        T("Two terms describe whether a fossil was moved. A **life assemblage** (biocoenosis) is a group of fossils preserved where the organisms lived: bivalves in burrowing position, both valves together, all sizes present. A **death assemblage** (thanatocoenosis) has been transported: single valves, broken shells, a narrow range of sizes and mixed species from different habitats."),
        H("Modes of preservation"),
        KEY("How remains are preserved", "From least changed to most changed.", cards=[
            ["Unaltered remains", "Original material kept: shells of young age, teeth, bones, insects in amber, mammoths frozen in ice, mummified remains in deserts."],
            ["Permineralization", "Minerals (silica, calcite, pyrite) are deposited in the pores of bone or wood; the original material stays. Petrified wood is the usual example."],
            ["Replacement", "The original material is dissolved and replaced, molecule by molecule, by another mineral: silicification (silica), pyritization (pyrite, in reducing conditions) or recrystallization of aragonite to calcite."],
            ["Carbonization", "Volatile parts are driven off and a thin film of carbon is left. Leaves, graptolites and soft-bodied animals in fine shales are preserved this way."],
            ["Moulds and casts", "A shell dissolves and leaves a hollow. An **external mould** records the outside; an **internal mould** (steinkern) is sediment that filled the inside. A **cast** forms when the hollow is later filled by a new mineral."],
            ["Trace fossils", "Evidence of activity, not of the body: tracks, trails, burrows, borings, coprolites (fossil dung). They are preserved where they formed, so they are never transported."],
        ]),
        T("A **Lagerstätte** (plural Lagerstätten) is a rock unit with exceptional preservation, often of soft parts. The Burgess Shale of Canada (Cambrian) is a standard example: animals were buried by mud flows in oxygen-poor water, so decay and scavenging were stopped. Lagerstätten show how much of ancient life is normally lost, because most organisms have no hard parts."),
        H("Assessing preservation potential"),
        KEY("Factors that raise preservation potential", "Use them as headings when a question names an organism.", cards=[
            ["Hard parts", "A mineralized skeleton (calcite, aragonite, silica, calcium phosphate) or a tough organic one (chitin) resists decay. Calcite is more stable than aragonite, which often dissolves or recrystallizes."],
            ["Habitat", "Shallow marine animals living where sediment accumulates are buried quickly. Animals on land, on hard rocky shores or in high-energy water are rarely buried."],
            ["Rapid burial and low oxygen", "Storm beds, turbidity currents, ash falls and anoxic bottom water stop scavengers and decay."],
            ["Abundance", "A common organism has more chances of being preserved than a rare one."],
            ["Robust shape and attachment", "A thick, compact shell resists breakage. An organism fixed to the sea floor is not carried away and broken by waves."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: preservation potential of a bivalve", intro="Assess the preservation potential of Cardium, a shallow marine burrowing bivalve with a calcareous shell.", steps=[
            S("State the verdict first", text="Cardium has a high preservation potential."),
            S("Hard parts", text="Its shell is calcium carbonate, which resists decay after the soft parts have gone."),
            S("Habitat and burial", text="It lived buried in sand or mud in shallow seas, where sediment accumulates, so it is already partly buried and is quickly covered."),
            S("Abundance", text="Bivalves of this type are common, so many individuals are available for preservation."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: reading an assemblage", intro="A sandstone bed contains single bivalve valves, all 2 to 3 cm long, most of them convex side up and many broken. Is it a life or a death assemblage? What does it tell you about the environment?", steps=[
            S("Evidence of transport", text="Separated valves, breakage and a narrow size range show sorting by currents."),
            S("Orientation", text="Convex-up valves are the stable position in a current, so they were laid down by moving water."),
            S("Conclusion", text="It is a death assemblage deposited by currents, in a high-energy environment such as a beach or a tidal channel."),
        ]),
        H("Taxonomy: naming fossils"),
        D("**Taxonomy** is the classification and naming of organisms. The ranks, from largest to smallest, are kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species. Each species has a two-part (binomial) name: the genus with a capital letter and the species in lower case, both in italics, for example *Paradoxides davidis*.", title="Taxonomy"),
        T("In palaeontology the **genus** is the usual working unit, because fossils rarely show the features used to separate living species. A **morphological species** is defined by shape alone. Phyla you meet in this module include Arthropoda (trilobites), Hemichordata (graptolites), Brachiopoda, Mollusca (bivalves, gastropods, cephalopods), Echinodermata (echinoids), Cnidaria (corals) and Chordata (vertebrates)."),
        H("Fossils as clues to past environments"),
        T("Living relatives and functional morphology let us read the environment of deposition. Reef-building corals indicate warm, clear, shallow sea water. Pyritized fossils point to reducing (oxygen-poor) conditions. Fossil trees with clear growth rings suggest seasonal climates; trees without rings suggest a uniform tropical climate. Plants with thick cuticles and sunken stomata suggest dry conditions. A diverse mixture of marine groups suggests a normal-salinity continental shelf."),
        MISTAKE("Giving reasons without a verdict", "The Board's examiners noted that many candidates explained why an organism is preserved but never said whether its preservation potential is high or low. Begin with the verdict, then give the reasons: skeleton, habitat, burial, abundance."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing a mould with a cast", "A mould is a hollow or an impression. A cast is a later filling of that hollow by new material. An internal mould (steinkern) is sediment that filled the inside of the shell."),
        QUIZ("A shell is dissolved after burial and the hollow left behind is later filled with calcite. What is the fossil called?", ["An external mould", "A cast", "A carbonized fossil", "A trace fossil"], 1,
             "The new calcite fills the hollow and reproduces the shape of the shell: a cast.", hint="Was a hollow filled by new material?",
             feedback=["An external mould is the empty impression, before filling.", "", "Carbonization leaves a carbon film from organic material.", "A trace fossil records activity such as a burrow, not the body."]),
        QUIZ("Which organism has the LOWEST preservation potential?", ["A brachiopod with a thick calcite shell in a shallow sea", "A jellyfish in open water", "An oyster cemented to a rock on a muddy sea floor", "A coral in a reef"], 1,
             "A jellyfish has no hard parts and lives where little sediment accumulates.", hint="Which one has no skeleton?",
             feedback=["Calcite shells in shallow seas are well preserved.", "", "Oysters have thick calcite shells and are common fossils.", "Coral skeletons of aragonite or calcite are common fossils."]),
        TIP("Taphonomy and preservation appear in Paper 1 (modes of preservation, trace fossils, assemblages) and in Paper 2 essays (preservation potential of named fossils, use of fossils in reconstructing environments). Paper 3 may ask you to describe how a specimen in front of you is preserved.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("(a) Define taphonomy. (b) Explain why the preservation potential of a trilobite is higher than that of a soft-bodied worm living in the same sea.",
           "(a) Taphonomy is the study of the processes that affect an organism from death to its discovery as a fossil: decay, disarticulation, transport, burial and diagenesis. (b) The trilobite has a hard exoskeleton of chitin strengthened with calcite, which resists decay and scavenging, so it survives long enough to be buried; the worm decays in days. Both live in shallow seas where burial is rapid, so the difference is the skeleton. The worm is preserved only in exceptional conditions such as a Lagerstätte."),
        PQ("Describe four modes of preservation of fossils, with an example of each.",
           "Unaltered remains: original shell or bone, insects in amber. Permineralization: minerals fill pores, as in petrified wood. Replacement: original material replaced by silica or pyrite, as in pyritized ammonites. Carbonization: a carbon film, as in graptolites or leaves in shale. Moulds and casts: a shell dissolves leaving an external mould; a cast forms when the hollow is filled. Trace fossils: burrows, tracks and coprolites."),
        PQ("Plan an essay: 'Explain how fossils help geologists to reconstruct the environments in which sedimentary rocks formed.'",
           "Introduction: fossils record organisms adapted to particular conditions; living relatives and functional morphology are the key. Paragraphs: reef corals indicate warm, clear, shallow marine water; pyritized fossils indicate reducing conditions; trees with growth rings indicate seasonal climates and those without rings tropical climates; plants with sunken stomata and thick cuticles indicate arid climates; a diverse marine fauna indicates a normal-salinity shelf; trace fossils such as deep vertical burrows indicate high-energy shores; life versus death assemblages indicate the energy of the water. Evaluation: the method assumes the organisms lived as their modern relatives do (uniformitarianism), which is less reliable for extinct groups; transport can move fossils out of their habitat. Conclusion: use several fossils and the sediment together."),
        SUM("Taphonomy runs from death through decay, disarticulation, transport and burial to diagenesis.",
            "Modes of preservation: unaltered remains, permineralization, replacement, carbonization, moulds and casts, and trace fossils.",
            "Preservation potential is raised by hard parts, a habitat where sediment accumulates, rapid burial, low oxygen and abundance.",
            "Life assemblages are preserved in place; death assemblages are transported, broken and sorted.",
            "Fossils are classified from kingdom to species and read as clues to past environments."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. Trilobites and graptolites
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_PAL,
    topic="Trilobites and graptolites",
    title="Trilobites and graptolites: morphology, modes of life and zone fossils",
    slug="gyu2-trilobites-graptolites",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Trilobites were Palaeozoic marine arthropods with a three-lobed exoskeleton; their eyes, spines and shape reveal how they lived. Graptolites were colonial planktonic hemichordates whose fast evolution of stipes and thecae makes them the classic zone fossils of the Ordovician and Silurian.",
    objectives=[
        "Label and describe the morphology of a trilobite and of a graptolite rhabdosome",
        "Infer the mode of life of a trilobite from its eyes, spines, shape and enrolment",
        "Describe the evolutionary trends in graptoloids from the Ordovician to the Silurian",
        "Explain why graptolites are better zone fossils than most trilobites",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Cephalon", "Glabella", "Facial suture", "Pygidium", "Moulting", "Rhabdosome", "Stipe", "Theca", "Sicula", "Zone fossil"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The oldest rocks with abundant fossils, from the Cambrian period, are full of trilobites. A few tens of millions of years later, black Ordovician and Silurian shales are covered with thin, saw-like carbon films: graptolites. Both groups died out long ago, yet they are among the most useful fossils for dating Palaeozoic rocks. This lesson goes beyond recognizing them: you will read their anatomy, infer how they lived and explain their value in biostratigraphy."),
        H("Trilobites"),
        D("**Trilobites** (phylum Arthropoda) were marine arthropods with a hard **exoskeleton** of chitin strengthened with calcite. The body is divided lengthways into three lobes (an axial lobe and two pleural lobes) and crossways into a head (**cephalon**), a segmented **thorax** and a tail shield (**pygidium**). Their range is Cambrian to Permian.", title="Trilobite"),
        IMG("algyu2-trilobite.svg", alt="Dorsal view of a trilobite with labels: cephalon, glabella, eye, facial suture, genal spine, thorax with axial and pleural lobes, pygidium",
            caption="Figure 1. Dorsal view of a generalized trilobite (schematic)."),
        KEY("Parts of the trilobite", "Learn each part with its function.", cards=[
            ["Cephalon", "Head shield. The raised central **glabella** covered the stomach. **Genal spines** at the back corners helped to spread the weight on soft mud."],
            ["Eyes and facial suture", "Compound eyes of calcite lenses sat on the free cheeks. The **facial suture** was a line of weakness that split during **moulting** (ecdysis), so the free cheeks came off."],
            ["Thorax", "A number of articulated segments, each with an axial ring and two pleurae. The joints allowed many trilobites to **enrol** (roll up) for protection."],
            ["Pygidium", "Fused tail segments. Its size compared with the cephalon changed during evolution: early forms had a small pygidium, later forms a larger one."],
        ]),
        T("Because trilobites moulted many times, one animal could leave several exoskeletons. Many trilobite fossils are therefore **moults**, not dead animals, which raises their preservation potential. The legs and gills were not mineralized and are preserved only in Lagerstätten."),
        H("Reading the mode of life of a trilobite"),
        KEY("Functional morphology", "Match the feature to the way of life.", cards=[
            ["Benthonic crawler", "Moderate eyes, flattened body, genal spines to spread the weight on the sea floor. Most trilobites, for example *Calymene* (Silurian)."],
            ["Burrower or deposit feeder", "Small or no eyes, a smooth convex shield, sometimes a broad fringe to sit on mud. *Trinucleus* (Ordovician) is blind with a pitted fringe."],
            ["Pelagic (swimming or floating)", "Very large eyes giving wide vision, slender spiny body, small size. Some, like the small blind agnostids with only two thoracic segments, are thought to have floated."],
            ["Active predator or scavenger", "Large eyes with big separate lenses (schizochroal eyes), as in *Phacops* (Devonian), and strong enrolment."],
        ]),
        T("Trilobites lived in many marine settings, so different species characterize different **faunal provinces**. Middle Cambrian *Paradoxides* lived on one side of the old Iapetus Ocean and *Olenellus* on the other. Their distribution is one line of evidence for the existence and closure of that ocean. This provincialism limits trilobites as zone fossils for worldwide correlation, but they are good zone fossils for the Cambrian within a province."),
        H("Graptolites"),
        D("**Graptolites** (phylum Hemichordata) were colonial marine animals. The colony, or **rhabdosome**, was a branching skeleton of organic material (collagen-like protein). Each branch is a **stipe**; along it the individuals lived in small cups called **thecae**. The colony grew from a first cone-shaped theca, the **sicula**, which hung from a thread, the **nema**.", title="Graptolite"),
        IMG("algyu2-graptolites.svg", alt="Five graptolite rhabdosomes in time order: a many-branched dendroid, a four-stiped form, a two-stiped tuning-fork form, a biserial form and a uniserial form",
            caption="Figure 2. Graptolite rhabdosomes arranged from the oldest (left) to the youngest (right). Schematic, not to scale."),
        T("Two orders matter. **Dendroids** (Middle Cambrian to Carboniferous) had many stipes joined by cross-bars and were mostly fixed to the sea floor. **Graptoloids** (Early Ordovician to Early Devonian) had few stipes and were planktonic: they floated in the upper water column, often attached to a float."),
        KEY("Evolutionary trends in graptoloids", "Each trend is one of the zone markers of the Ordovician and Silurian.", cards=[
            ["Fewer stipes", "Many (*Dictyonema*, a planktonic dendroid at the base of the Ordovician), then eight, four (*Tetragraptus*), two (*Didymograptus*, tuning fork), finally one."],
            ["Stipe position", "The stipes moved from hanging down (pendent) through horizontal to pointing up (reclined), and finally grew back to back up the nema (scandent): biserial *Diplograptus* (Ordovician)."],
            ["Uniserial colonies", "In the Silurian, *Monograptus* had a single stipe with thecae on one side only; it lasted into the Early Devonian."],
            ["Thecal shape", "Thecae changed from simple tubes to hooked, lobed or isolated forms, as in *Rastrites* (Silurian) with widely separated thecae."],
        ]),
        T("Graptolites are the classic **zone fossils** of the Ordovician and Silurian because they evolved fast (each species lasted a short time), were planktonic (spread quickly across oceans, in many facies), were abundant and are easily recognized. Their weakness is preservation: as carbon films they survive best in black, anoxic shales and are rare in limestones and sandstones."),
        EX("Worked example 1: dating with graptolites", intro="A black shale contains abundant two-stiped graptolites with horizontal stipes and no biserial or uniserial forms. A second shale higher in the sequence contains only uniserial graptolites. Suggest the ages of the two shales.", steps=[
            S("Two-stiped horizontal forms", text="This is the *Didymograptus* type, typical of the Lower to Middle Ordovician."),
            S("Uniserial forms only", text="*Monograptus*-type colonies characterize the Silurian (to Early Devonian)."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The lower shale is Ordovician and the upper shale is Silurian, consistent with superposition. Check exact zone names in your textbook."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: interpreting a trilobite", intro="A trilobite has very large eyes on the cephalon, long genal spines and a narrow, spiny body. Suggest its mode of life and give reasons.", steps=[
            S("Eyes", text="Very large eyes give a wide field of view, used by an active animal in the water column."),
            S("Body", text="A narrow, spiny, light body reduces sinking and suits swimming or floating."),
            S("Conclusion", text="A pelagic (swimming) mode of life. A blind, smooth, convex form would instead suggest burrowing."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling graptolites corals or plants", "Graptolites are colonial hemichordates, related to living pterobranchs. Their saw-tooth outline comes from the thecae, not from leaves or septa."),
        MISTAKE("Describing the trilobite as three segments", "The name refers to three lobes along the body (one axial, two pleural). The three parts from front to back are cephalon, thorax and pygidium: two different divisions."),
        QUIZ("Which feature allowed a trilobite to shed its exoskeleton during moulting?", ["The glabella", "The facial suture", "The genal spine", "The pygidium"], 1,
             "The facial suture is a line of weakness across the cephalon that opened during moulting.", hint="Which feature is a line of weakness?",
             feedback=["The glabella is the raised central part over the stomach.", "", "Genal spines spread the weight on soft sediment.", "The pygidium is the fused tail shield."]),
        QUIZ("Why are graptoloids such good zone fossils?", ["They lived fixed to the sea floor in reefs", "They evolved rapidly and floated widely across the oceans", "They have thick calcite skeletons that are always preserved", "They range from the Cambrian to the present day"], 1,
             "Rapid evolution gives short ranges; a planktonic life gives wide distribution.", hint="What two qualities does a zone fossil need?",
             feedback=["Graptoloids were planktonic, not reef dwellers.", "", "Their skeleton was organic and preserved mainly as carbon films.", "They became extinct in the Devonian."]),
        TIP("Paper 1 often tests the parts of a trilobite and the order of graptolite forms. Paper 3 can give you a specimen or drawing to identify, describe and date. Draw large, label neatly and give the geological range with the reason.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Draw and label the dorsal view of a trilobite. State the function of the facial suture and of the genal spines.",
           "Labels: cephalon, glabella, eye, facial suture, free cheek, fixed cheek, genal spine, thorax (axial lobe and pleural lobes, segments), pygidium. The facial suture split during moulting so that the animal could leave its old exoskeleton. Genal spines spread the weight of the animal on soft mud and may have helped stability or defence."),
        PQ("Describe the main evolutionary trends shown by graptoloids from the Ordovician to the Silurian.",
           "Reduction in the number of stipes from many (dendroids such as Dictyonema) to eight, four (Tetragraptus), two (Didymograptus) and one (Monograptus). Change in stipe attitude from pendent through horizontal and reclined to scandent, giving biserial forms (Diplograptus) in which the stipes grew back to back. In the Silurian, uniserial monograptids with thecae on one side. Increasing complexity of thecae, for example isolated thecae in Rastrites."),
        PQ("Evaluate the usefulness of trilobites and graptolites in the correlation of Palaeozoic rocks.",
           "Graptolites: rapid evolution, short ranges, planktonic so found worldwide and across facies, abundant: excellent for the Ordovician and Silurian; limited by poor preservation outside black shales and by their absence after the Early Devonian. Trilobites: abundant and varied through the Cambrian, easily recognized, high preservation potential because of moulting; but most were benthonic, so faunas were provincial and facies-controlled, which limits long-distance correlation. Conclusion: graptolites are better for global correlation of the Ordovician-Silurian, trilobites for the Cambrian within a faunal province."),
        SUM("A trilobite has three lobes along the body and a cephalon, thorax and pygidium; the facial suture opened during moulting.",
            "Eyes, spines, shape and enrolment reveal benthonic, burrowing, pelagic or predatory modes of life.",
            "Graptolites are colonial hemichordates: rhabdosome, stipes, thecae, sicula and nema.",
            "Graptoloid trends: fewer stipes, from pendent to scandent, biserial then uniserial colonies, more complex thecae.",
            "Graptolites are excellent Ordovician-Silurian zone fossils; trilobites are best for the Cambrian within a province."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Brachiopods and bivalves
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_PAL,
    topic="Brachiopods and bivalves",
    title="Brachiopods and bivalves: symmetry, morphology and modes of life",
    slug="gyu2-brachiopods-bivalves",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Brachiopods and bivalves both have two shells but are built differently: a brachiopod's plane of symmetry cuts through each valve, a bivalve's lies between the valves. Muscle scars, hinge teeth, the pallial sinus and shell shape reveal how each lived.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish brachiopods from bivalves using the plane of symmetry and internal features",
        "Describe the morphology of an articulate brachiopod and of a bivalve",
        "Infer the mode of life of a bivalve from shell shape, pallial sinus and attachment features",
        "Explain the change from brachiopod-dominated to bivalve-dominated shelf faunas",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Pedicle valve", "Brachial valve", "Lophophore", "Commissure", "Umbo", "Hinge teeth", "Adductor muscle scar", "Pallial sinus", "Byssus"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("On a Kribi beach you can collect clam and cockle shells: two hinged valves that close over a soft animal. In Palaeozoic limestones the commonest two-valved fossils look similar but belong to a different phylum, the brachiopods. Examiners regularly ask you to tell them apart and to explain how each lived. The key is symmetry, then the internal features."),
        H("Two ways to build a two-valved shell"),
        IMG("algyu2-symmetry.svg", alt="Left: brachiopod seen from above with a plane of symmetry running down the middle of each valve. Right: bivalve seen from above with the plane of symmetry between the two valves along the hinge line",
            caption="Figure 1. The plane of symmetry in a brachiopod (left) and in a bivalve (right). Schematic."),
        KEY("The symmetry rule", "This is the first thing to state in any comparison.", cards=[
            ["Brachiopod", "Valves are **unequal** (one larger than the other), but **each valve is symmetrical** about a plane that runs from the beak to the front margin, through both valves."],
            ["Bivalve", "Valves are usually **equal** (mirror images of each other); the plane of symmetry lies **between** the valves along the hinge line, so each valve on its own is usually not symmetrical."],
        ]),
        H("Brachiopods"),
        D("**Brachiopods** (phylum Brachiopoda) are marine animals with two unequal valves. The larger **pedicle valve** usually has a hole (**foramen**) for a fleshy stalk, the **pedicle**, that fixed the animal to the sea floor. The smaller **brachial valve** carried the **lophophore**, a ring of tentacles used for filter feeding, often supported by a calcareous **brachidium** (for example a spiral in *Spirifer*). The line where the two valves meet is the **commissure**. Range: Cambrian to Recent.", title="Brachiopod"),
        KEY("Articulate and inarticulate brachiopods", "Two main groups.", cards=[
            ["Articulate", "Calcite shell; the valves hinge with **teeth** in the pedicle valve fitting **sockets** in the brachial valve. Opened by diductor muscles and closed by adductor muscles. Examples: *Spirifer* and *Productus* (Carboniferous), *Terebratula*."],
            ["Inarticulate", "Shell of chitin and calcium phosphate, no teeth; valves held only by muscles. *Lingula* burrows in mud and has lived almost unchanged since the Cambrian, a 'living fossil'."],
        ]),
        T("Brachiopods were filter feeders living on the sea floor. Most were attached by the pedicle. Some, like the productids, lost the pedicle and rested on the mud, held by spines. A **folded commissure** (a zigzag or a fold and sulcus) separated incoming and outgoing water currents and kept out sediment grains, which suits muddy water."),
        H("Bivalves"),
        D("**Bivalves** (phylum Mollusca, class Bivalvia, also called lamellibranchs or pelecypods) have two valves, usually equal, of aragonite or calcite, joined at the hinge by a **ligament** and **hinge teeth**. The beak, or **umbo**, is the oldest part of each valve. Inside, one or two **adductor muscle scars** mark the muscles that closed the shell, and the **pallial line** marks the edge of the mantle. Range: Cambrian to Recent.", title="Bivalve"),
        KEY("Reading a bivalve's mode of life", "Shell shape and internal marks reveal behaviour.", cards=[
            ["Shallow burrower", "Thick, rounded, ribbed shell; short siphons, so little or no **pallial sinus**; strong hinge teeth. *Cardium* (cockles)."],
            ["Deep burrower", "Elongated, smooth shell, a deep pallial sinus (a notch in the pallial line where long siphons were withdrawn) and a gape where the siphons stuck out. *Mya*."],
            ["Byssally attached", "Fixed to rock by threads (the byssus) from a notch; shape often triangular. *Mytilus* (mussel)."],
            ["Cemented", "One valve cemented to the sea floor, irregular and thick; unequal valves. Oysters such as *Ostrea*; *Gryphaea* (Jurassic) lay free on the mud with one curved valve."],
            ["Free-swimming", "Thin, flat, light valves with 'ears' beside the hinge; swims by clapping the valves. *Pecten* (scallop)."],
            ["Borer", "Rasping ridges at the front of the shell; lives in bored holes in rock or wood."],
        ]),
        CODE("""
Feature              Brachiopod                      Bivalve
-------------------  ------------------------------  ------------------------------
Phylum               Brachiopoda                     Mollusca
Plane of symmetry    through each valve              between the valves
Valves               unequal (pedicle, brachial)     usually equal, left and right
Feeding organ        lophophore (often brachidium)   gills
Attachment           pedicle through a foramen       byssus, cement, or free
Muscle scars         diductor and adductor           1 or 2 adductor scars
Mantle marks         none like a pallial line        pallial line, pallial sinus
Dominant on shelves  Palaeozoic                      Mesozoic and Cenozoic
""", "text", title="Table 1. Brachiopods and bivalves compared"),
        H("Evolution: a change of the guard"),
        T("Brachiopods dominated Palaeozoic shelf seas. The **end-Permian mass extinction** (about 252 million years ago) removed most brachiopod groups. In the Mesozoic and Cenozoic the bivalves, which could burrow deeply using siphons and so escape predators, took over most shelf habitats. Whether bivalves out-competed brachiopods or simply recovered better after the extinction is still debated."),
        EX("Worked example 1: brachiopod or bivalve?", intro="A fossil has two valves of different sizes. The larger valve has a hole near the beak. A line drawn from the beak to the front margin divides each valve into two mirror-image halves. Identify the group.", steps=[
            S("Unequal valves", text="Unequal valves point to a brachiopod (or an oyster)."),
            S("Hole near the beak", text="This is the pedicle foramen of the pedicle valve."),
            S("Symmetry", text="Each valve is symmetrical about a plane through both valves: the brachiopod pattern."),
            S("Conclusion", text="An articulate brachiopod. An oyster would be irregular, with no foramen and no such symmetry."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: mode of life of a bivalve", intro="A bivalve is elongated and smooth. Inside, the pallial line has a deep notch at the back, and the valves do not close at the back end. Suggest its mode of life.", steps=[
            S("Pallial sinus", text="A deep pallial sinus shows long siphons that could be pulled in."),
            S("Gape", text="The posterior gape is where the siphons were permanently extended."),
            S("Shape", text="Elongated, smooth valves slide easily through sediment."),
            S("Conclusion", text="A deep burrower in soft sediment, feeding through long siphons that reached the sea floor."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Stating that bivalves are symmetrical and brachiopods are not", "Both are symmetrical; the difference is where the plane lies. Draw and label it: through each valve for brachiopods, between the valves for bivalves."),
        MISTAKE("Giving gills to brachiopods", "Brachiopods feed with a lophophore; bivalves feed with gills. Do not mix the feeding organs."),
        QUIZ("Which feature of a bivalve shows that it had long siphons?", ["A large umbo", "A deep pallial sinus", "Strong ribs on the outer surface", "Two adductor muscle scars"], 1,
             "The pallial sinus is the notch in the pallial line where the siphon-withdrawing muscles were attached.", hint="Look at the pallial line.",
             feedback=["The umbo is the beak, the oldest part of the valve.", "", "Ribs strengthen the shell and suit shallow burrowers.", "Two adductor scars are found in many bivalves, with or without siphons."]),
        QUIZ("A two-valved fossil has a spiral calcareous support inside the smaller valve. What is it?", ["The brachidium of a brachiopod", "The hinge teeth of a bivalve", "The siphuncle of a cephalopod", "The pallial sinus of a bivalve"], 0,
             "A spiral brachidium supported the lophophore, as in Spirifer.", hint="Which group has a feeding organ supported by calcite?",
             feedback=["", "Hinge teeth lie along the hinge line and are not spiral.", "The siphuncle is a tube through the chambers of a cephalopod.", "The pallial sinus is a mark on the inner surface, not a support."]),
        TIP("The brachiopod-bivalve comparison is a favourite in Paper 1 and Paper 2. In Paper 3 you may be shown a specimen and asked for its group, mode of life and environment: state the evidence (symmetry, pallial sinus, attachment) for every answer.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("With labelled diagrams, distinguish between a brachiopod and a bivalve.",
           "Brachiopod diagram: pedicle valve with foramen and beak, brachial valve, commissure, plane of symmetry through both valves, internal brachidium and muscle scars. Bivalve diagram: two equal valves, umbo, hinge line with teeth and ligament, two adductor muscle scars, pallial line and sinus, plane of symmetry between valves. Differences: position of the plane of symmetry; unequal versus equal valves; lophophore versus gills; pedicle versus byssus or burrowing; brachiopods are Brachiopoda, bivalves are Mollusca."),
        PQ("Describe how the shell of a bivalve can be used to deduce whether it was a burrower, a swimmer or attached to rock.",
           "Burrowers: thick ribbed rounded shells (shallow burrowers) or elongated smooth shells with a deep pallial sinus and gape (deep burrowers). Swimmers: thin, flat, light valves with ears beside the hinge, such as Pecten. Attached forms: a byssal notch (Mytilus) or one valve cemented and irregular (oysters). Borers: rasping ridges and a shape fitting a boring."),
        PQ("Suggest why brachiopods became less important than bivalves after the Palaeozoic.",
           "The end-Permian mass extinction removed most brachiopod groups. Bivalves survived better and diversified. Siphons allowed bivalves to burrow deeply and escape predators, which became more numerous in the Mesozoic. Bivalves could also live in more habitats (burrowing, boring, swimming, brackish water). The relative role of competition and extinction is still debated."),
        SUM("Brachiopods: unequal valves, each symmetrical; bivalves: equal valves, plane of symmetry between them.",
            "Brachiopods have pedicle and brachial valves, a lophophore and often a brachidium; articulate forms have teeth and sockets.",
            "Bivalves have an umbo, hinge teeth, a ligament, adductor scars, a pallial line and sometimes a pallial sinus.",
            "Shell shape, pallial sinus, byssal notch and cementation reveal burrowing, swimming or attached modes of life.",
            "Brachiopods dominated Palaeozoic shelves; bivalves dominated after the end-Permian extinction."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. Gastropods and cephalopods
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_PAL,
    topic="Gastropods and cephalopods",
    title="Gastropods and cephalopods: coiled shells, sutures and belemnites",
    slug="gyu2-gastropods-cephalopods",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Gastropods have a single, usually spiral, undivided shell; cephalopods have a chambered shell crossed by a siphuncle. The pattern of the suture line separates nautiloids, goniatites, ceratites and ammonites, which makes ammonoids superb zone fossils, and belemnites keep only a solid internal guard.",
    objectives=[
        "Label the shell of a gastropod and infer its feeding and habitat from the aperture",
        "Describe the chambered shell of a cephalopod: septa, sutures, siphuncle and body chamber",
        "Distinguish nautiloid, goniatitic, ceratitic and ammonitic sutures and give their age ranges",
        "Explain why ammonoids are excellent zone fossils and describe a belemnite",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Whorl", "Aperture", "Siphonal canal", "Septum", "Suture line", "Siphuncle", "Saddle and lobe", "Ammonoid", "Belemnite guard"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Snails in a Bamenda garden and the land snails sold in markets are **gastropods**. Their marine cousins, the cephalopods, include today's squid, octopus and pearly *Nautilus*. Both are molluscs, and both can build coiled shells. But a coiled snail shell is one open tube, while a coiled ammonite is divided into chambers by walls. That difference, and the shapes of those walls, is the heart of this lesson."),
        H("Gastropods"),
        D("**Gastropods** (phylum Mollusca, class Gastropoda) have a single shell, usually coiled in a helical spiral around a central axis (the **columella**), with no internal walls. The shell is made of **whorls**; the last and largest is the **body whorl**, which opens at the **aperture**. The older whorls form the **spire**, ending at the **apex**, and the lines where whorls meet are **sutures**. Range: Cambrian to Recent.", title="Gastropod"),
        KEY("Reading a gastropod shell", "The aperture tells you how it fed.", cards=[
            ["Siphonal canal", "A groove at the base of the aperture that held a siphon. It shows a **carnivore** or scavenger that 'smelled' prey; many such forms also plough through or burrow in soft sediment."],
            ["Entire (round) aperture", "No canal: typical of **herbivores** grazing on algae on rock or plants."],
            ["Dextral or sinistral", "With the apex up and the aperture facing you, a dextral shell opens on the right, a sinistral one on the left. Most are dextral."],
            ["Habitats", "Gastropods live in the sea, in fresh water and on land, so they are poor indicators of salinity on their own; the shell form must be compared with modern relatives."],
        ]),
        T("Gastropods are abundant and diverse in Cenozoic rocks, for example the high-spired *Turritella*. They are not good zone fossils: many genera have long ranges, they live in many facies, and shells of different ages look alike."),
        H("Cephalopods"),
        D("**Cephalopods** (class Cephalopoda) are marine molluscs with tentacles. Shelled forms have a conical shell, straight or coiled, divided by walls called **septa** into **chambers** (camerae). The animal lived in the last, open **body chamber**. A tube, the **siphuncle**, passed through all the chambers; by removing or adding liquid through it, the animal controlled the gas and liquid in the chambers and so its buoyancy. The line where a septum meets the shell wall is the **suture line**.", title="Cephalopod"),
        T("Sutures are seen when the outer shell has been removed, as on many internal moulds. A suture is described by its **saddles** (folds that point forward, towards the aperture) and **lobes** (folds that point back, towards the apex). The complexity of the suture increased through time, and it identifies the main groups."),
        IMG("algyu2-sutures.svg", alt="Four suture lines: a straight or gently curved nautiloid suture, a goniatitic suture with smooth zigzag saddles and lobes, a ceratitic suture with smooth saddles and frilled lobes, and an ammonitic suture with frilled saddles and lobes",
            caption="Figure 1. Suture types drawn flat. The arrow shows the direction of the aperture. Schematic."),
        KEY("Cephalopod groups and their sutures", "Learn the suture with the range.", cards=[
            ["Nautiloids", "Straight or gently curved sutures; septa concave towards the aperture; siphuncle usually central. Straight *Orthoceras*; coiled *Nautilus*. Range: Cambrian to Recent."],
            ["Goniatites", "Ammonoids with simple angular zigzag sutures, smooth saddles and lobes. Devonian to Permian."],
            ["Ceratites", "Smooth, rounded saddles but frilled (serrated) lobes. Mainly Triassic."],
            ["Ammonites", "Both saddles and lobes are finely frilled, a very complex suture. Mainly Jurassic and Cretaceous; all ammonoids died out at the end of the Cretaceous."],
        ]),
        T("Ammonoids (goniatites, ceratites and ammonites) differ from nautiloids in more than the suture: their siphuncle runs along the outer (ventral) margin, and their septa are convex towards the aperture. Many ammonites also carry ribs, keels and tubercles, and the coiling may be **evolute** (all whorls visible) or **involute** (the last whorl covers the earlier ones)."),
        T("Ammonites are perhaps the best zone fossils of all. They evolved very fast, so a species often lasted well under a million years; they swam or drifted widely, so the same species occurs on different continents; they are abundant, varied and easy to tell apart. The Jurassic is divided into many ammonite zones (check your textbook for examples)."),
        H("Belemnites"),
        D("**Belemnites** were squid-like cephalopods with an **internal** skeleton. The commonly fossilized part is the solid, bullet-shaped **guard** (rostrum) of radiating calcite fibres. In front of it sat the **phragmocone**, a small chambered cone with septa and a siphuncle, and a thin plate, the **pro-ostracum**. Range: mainly Jurassic and Cretaceous.", title="Belemnite"),
        T("Because the guard is dense calcite, belemnites have a high preservation potential and their oxygen isotopes have been used to estimate Mesozoic sea temperatures. Fossil 'belemnite battlefields' (many guards together) are probably the result of mass mortality after mating, or of predators."),
        EX("Worked example 1: identifying a cephalopod", intro="A coiled internal mould shows sutures with smooth rounded saddles and finely toothed lobes, and a siphuncle on the outer margin. Identify it and give its likely age.", steps=[
            S("Siphuncle on the outer margin", text="This is an ammonoid, not a nautiloid."),
            S("Smooth saddles, frilled lobes", text="That is a ceratitic suture."),
            S("Conclusion", text="A ceratite, most likely Triassic in age."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: gastropod or ammonite?", intro="A student finds a coiled fossil shell. Explain how she can tell whether it is a gastropod or an ammonite.", steps=[
            S("Coiling", text="A gastropod coils helically around an axis, so it has a spire and is not symmetrical; an ammonite coils in one plane (planispiral) and is bilaterally symmetrical."),
            S("Chambers", text="Break or section the shell: an ammonite has septa and a siphuncle; a gastropod is one undivided tube."),
            S("Sutures", text="Suture lines crossing the whorls are septal sutures in an ammonite; in a gastropod the sutures are only the lines between whorls."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using 'suture' in the wrong group", "In a gastropod the suture is the line where two whorls meet. In a cephalopod the suture is where a septum meets the outer wall. Say which you mean."),
        MISTAKE("Saying all coiled shells are ammonites", "Some gastropods and some nautiloids are coiled. Check the plane of coiling, the septa, the position of the siphuncle and the suture pattern."),
        QUIZ("Which suture type has frilled saddles AND frilled lobes?", ["Nautiloid", "Goniatitic", "Ceratitic", "Ammonitic"], 3,
             "Only the ammonitic suture is frilled throughout.", hint="Which is the most complex?",
             feedback=["Nautiloid sutures are straight or gently curved.", "Goniatitic sutures are angular but smooth.", "Ceratitic sutures have smooth saddles and frilled lobes only.", ""]),
        QUIZ("What did the siphuncle of a cephalopod do?", ["It held the gills", "It controlled the gas and liquid in the chambers for buoyancy", "It carried food to the stomach", "It attached the animal to the sea floor"], 1,
             "The siphuncle removed or added liquid in the chambers, adjusting buoyancy.", hint="Why does a chambered shell float?",
             feedback=["The gills were in the body chamber.", "", "Food was eaten through the mouth in the body chamber.", "Cephalopods swam; they were not attached."]),
        TIP("Paper 1 likes suture types and ranges. In Paper 2 and Paper 3 you may be asked why ammonites are good zone fossils, or to give the mode of life of a gastropod from its aperture: name the feature (siphonal canal) as your reason.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Distinguish between a nautiloid and an ammonoid.",
           "Nautiloid: simple straight or gently curved sutures, septa concave towards the aperture, siphuncle usually central, range Cambrian to Recent. Ammonoid: complex sutures (goniatitic, ceratitic or ammonitic), septa convex towards the aperture, siphuncle on the outer (ventral) margin, range Devonian to Cretaceous."),
        PQ("Explain why ammonites are considered excellent zone fossils for the Jurassic.",
           "They evolved rapidly, so each species had a short range; they were swimmers or drifters, so they spread widely across seas and are found on several continents; they are abundant in many marine sediments; their sutures, ribbing and coiling make species easy to recognize. Limitations: they are rare in non-marine rocks, and shells can drift after death into other environments."),
        PQ("Describe the morphology of a belemnite and explain why it is common as a fossil.",
           "Belemnites had an internal skeleton: a solid bullet-shaped guard of radiating calcite fibres, a chambered phragmocone with septa and siphuncle in front of it, and a thin pro-ostracum. The guard is dense, stable calcite and was buried in marine muds, so it has a high preservation potential, while the soft body and the phragmocone are rarely preserved."),
        SUM("Gastropods have one undivided, usually helical shell: apex, spire, whorls, body whorl and aperture.",
            "A siphonal canal suggests a carnivore; an entire aperture suggests a grazer.",
            "Cephalopod shells are chambered by septa and pierced by a siphuncle; the suture line marks each septum.",
            "Sutures: nautiloid (simple), goniatitic (Devonian-Permian), ceratitic (Triassic), ammonitic (Jurassic-Cretaceous).",
            "Ammonites are superb zone fossils; belemnites leave a dense calcite guard."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Echinoids and corals
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_PAL,
    topic="Echinoids and corals",
    title="Echinoids and corals: symmetry, adaptation and environmental indicators",
    slug="gyu2-echinoids-corals",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Regular echinoids are five-fold symmetrical grazers living on the sea floor; irregular echinoids added a front and back and became burrowers. Rugose, tabulate and scleractinian corals differ in their septa and ranges, and reef corals are strong indicators of warm, clear, shallow water.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the test of an echinoid and distinguish regular from irregular echinoids",
        "Relate the features of irregular echinoids, including Micraster, to a burrowing life",
        "Distinguish rugose, tabulate and scleractinian corals and give their ranges",
        "Use corals to interpret environments and growth lines to estimate days in an ancient year",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Test", "Ambulacrum", "Periproct", "Peristome", "Regular echinoid", "Irregular echinoid", "Septa", "Tabulae", "Rugose", "Scleractinian"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A sea urchin on a rocky shore near Limbe is covered in spines and has its mouth underneath. A heart urchin buried in sand is lopsided and almost spineless. Both are **echinoids**, and the difference between them tells a story of adaptation. Corals tell a different story: where they grow, the water must be warm, clear and shallow, so fossil reefs are thermometers of the past."),
        H("Echinoids"),
        D("**Echinoids** (phylum Echinodermata, class Echinoidea) have a rigid globular or flattened **test** of interlocking calcite plates, covered in life by spines attached to **tubercles**. The test has five double rows of perforated plates, the **ambulacra**, through which the tube feet passed (in pore pairs), separated by five **interambulacra**. The mouth opens at the **peristome** and the anus at the **periproct**. Range: Ordovician to Recent.", title="Echinoid"),
        IMG("algyu2-echinoids.svg", alt="Upper views of two echinoids. Left: a round regular echinoid with five straight ambulacra and the periproct at the centre of the apical system. Right: a heart-shaped irregular echinoid with petal-shaped ambulacra, a front groove and the periproct at the back",
            caption="Figure 1. Regular (left) and irregular (right) echinoids from above. Schematic."),
        KEY("Regular and irregular echinoids", "Symmetry again comes first.", cards=[
            ["Regular", "Round test with **five-fold radial (pentameral) symmetry**. Periproct inside the **apical system** on top; peristome in the middle underneath, with jaws (**Aristotle's lantern**). Large tubercles and spines. Lived on the sea floor grazing algae. Example: *Cidaris*."],
            ["Irregular", "**Bilateral symmetry** laid over the five-fold pattern: a front and a back. Periproct moved out of the apical system to the back; peristome often moved forward; ambulacra on top often **petaloid**. Small spines, usually no lantern. Burrowed in sediment as deposit feeders. Examples: *Micraster*, *Clypeus*."],
        ]),
        T("The Late Cretaceous heart urchin ***Micraster***, from the Chalk of Europe, is a classic example of evolution in fossils. Through the Chalk succession its test became broader and higher, the mouth moved forward and became covered by a lip (labrum), and the surface became more granular. These changes are usually read as adaptations to deeper burrowing. Textbooks describe the details differently, so check your textbook."),
        H("Corals"),
        D("**Corals** (phylum Cnidaria, class Anthozoa) are polyps that build a calcareous skeleton. Each polyp sits in a cup, the **corallite** (calyx), divided by radial plates called **septa**. Horizontal plates, **tabulae**, close off older parts of the corallite. A coral may be **solitary** (one corallite, often horn-shaped) or **colonial** (a colony of many corallites, the corallum).", title="Coral"),
        KEY("Three orders of corals", "Septa and range separate them.", cards=[
            ["Rugose", "Solitary or colonial; septa inserted in four places, giving **bilateral** arrangement; wrinkled outer wall. Solitary horn coral *Zaphrentis*, colonial *Lithostrotion* (Carboniferous). Ordovician to Permian."],
            ["Tabulate", "Always colonial; prominent **tabulae**, septa short or absent; walls often with pores. *Favosites* (honeycomb), *Halysites* (chain coral). Ordovician to Permian."],
            ["Scleractinian", "Solitary or colonial; septa in multiples of **six** (hexameral); aragonite skeleton. All modern reef corals. Middle Triassic to Recent."],
        ]),
        T("Modern **reef-building** (hermatypic) corals contain symbiotic algae (zooxanthellae) that need light. They therefore live in **warm** water (roughly above 18 to 20 °C), **clear** water free of mud, **shallow** water in the well-lit zone (usually within a few tens of metres of the surface) and **normal marine salinity**. A fossil reef of similar corals points to the same conditions. Solitary corals without algae can live in cold or deep water, so use reef-building forms as indicators."),
        H("Corals as clocks"),
        T("Coral walls show very fine growth ridges, probably one per day, grouped into annual bands. In the 1960s the American palaeontologist John Wells counted about 400 daily ridges per year in Devonian corals. Because the Earth's rotation is slowed by tidal friction, there were more, shorter days in a Devonian year. The result agreed with astronomical estimates."),
        EX("Worked example 1: days in an ancient year", intro="Five annual bands on a fossil coral contain 1 990 daily growth ridges in total (teaching figures, invented for practice). Estimate the number of days in the year and the length of a day, assuming the year lasted as long as today (about 8 766 hours).", steps=[
            S("Days per year", math=r"1\,990 \div 5 = 398 \text{ days}"),
            S("Length of one day", math=r"8\,766 \div 398 \approx 22.0 \text{ hours}"),
            S("Interpretation", text="More, shorter days than today: the Earth spun faster, consistent with a Palaeozoic age."),
        ], check=r"398 \times 22.0 \approx 8\,756 \approx 8\,766"),
        EX("Worked example 2: naming a coral", intro="A colonial coral has closely packed polygonal corallites with many horizontal tabulae, pores in the walls and almost no septa. Name the order and give its range.", steps=[
            S("Colonial with prominent tabulae and reduced septa", text="These are the features of a tabulate coral."),
            S("Polygonal corallites with wall pores", text="This fits *Favosites*, the honeycomb coral."),
            S("Range", text="Tabulate corals: Ordovician to Permian, so the rock is Palaeozoic."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Writing 'shallow marine' for corals", "The Board's examiners noted candidates who wrote only 'shallow marine' for a reef coral. The full environmental significance is **warm, clear, shallow marine** water of normal salinity."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the apical system and the peristome", "The apical system is on top (with the genital plates and, in regular echinoids, the periproct). The peristome, with the mouth, is underneath."),
        QUIZ("In which echinoid is the periproct outside the apical system?", ["A regular echinoid such as Cidaris", "An irregular echinoid such as Micraster", "All echinoids", "No echinoid"], 1,
             "In irregular echinoids the periproct has moved to the posterior margin.", hint="Which group has a front and a back?",
             feedback=["In regular echinoids the periproct is inside the apical system.", "", "Regular echinoids keep it in the apical system.", "Irregular echinoids show this clearly."]),
        QUIZ("A coral has septa in multiples of six and an aragonite skeleton. Which order is it?", ["Rugose", "Tabulate", "Scleractinian", "Graptoloid"], 2,
             "Hexameral septa and aragonite identify scleractinian corals (Triassic to Recent).", hint="Which order includes modern reef corals?",
             feedback=["Rugose septa are inserted in four places.", "Tabulate corals have reduced or absent septa.", "", "Graptoloids are not corals at all."]),
        TIP("Paper 1 tests the differences between coral orders and between regular and irregular echinoids. Paper 2 essays ask how fossils indicate past environments: corals are your strongest example. Paper 3 may ask for the environmental significance of a coral specimen.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Compare a regular and an irregular echinoid and relate the differences to their modes of life.",
           "Regular: pentameral symmetry, periproct in the apical system, central peristome with Aristotle's lantern, large tubercles and spines: an epifaunal grazer on hard or firm sea floor. Irregular: bilateral symmetry imposed on the five-fold plan, periproct at the back, peristome moved forward, petaloid ambulacra for respiration, small dense spines, no lantern: an infaunal burrowing deposit feeder. The changes keep waste behind the animal and help it move forward through sediment."),
        PQ("State the conditions needed by modern reef-building corals and explain how fossil corals are used to reconstruct past environments.",
           "Warm water (roughly above 18 to 20 °C), clear water with little suspended sediment, shallow well-lit water (the algae need light), normal marine salinity and a firm base. A fossil reef of similar corals suggests the same conditions, so it indicates a warm shallow tropical sea, and the position of ancient reefs helps locate old tropical latitudes. Limitation: older (rugose and tabulate) corals are extinct, so we assume similar needs (uniformitarianism)."),
        PQ("A Carboniferous coral shows 2 340 daily growth ridges in six annual bands (invented teaching figures). Calculate the number of days in the year and comment.",
           "2 340 ÷ 6 = 390 days per year. This is more than today's 365, so days were shorter and the Earth rotated faster; the rotation has slowed through tidal friction since the Carboniferous."),
        SUM("Echinoids have a calcite test with five ambulacra and five interambulacra, a peristome and a periproct.",
            "Regular echinoids are pentameral grazers; irregular echinoids are bilateral burrowers with the periproct at the back.",
            "Micraster in the Chalk shows gradual changes linked to deeper burrowing (check details in your textbook).",
            "Rugose (four-fold insertion) and tabulate (tabulae, few septa) corals are Palaeozoic; scleractinians (six-fold) are Triassic to Recent.",
            "Reef corals indicate warm, clear, shallow, normal-salinity seas; growth ridges record days per year."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Microfossils, plants and vertebrates
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_PAL,
    topic="Microfossils, plants and vertebrates",
    title="Microfossils, plant fossils, vertebrates and the evolution of life",
    slug="gyu2-microfossils-plants-vertebrates",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Microfossils (foraminifera, radiolaria, diatoms, ostracods, coccoliths, conodonts, spores and pollen) survive drilling and date oil wells. Plant and vertebrate fossils record life on land, past climates and continental drift, and the record shows evolution and mass extinctions.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the main groups of microfossils and explain why they are used in petroleum exploration",
        "Describe Carboniferous coal-forest plants and use plants as indicators of climate and continental drift",
        "Outline the main steps in vertebrate history and the major mass extinctions",
        "Compare phyletic gradualism with punctuated equilibrium using fossil evidence",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Microfossil", "Foraminifera", "Palynology", "Conodont", "Lepidodendron", "Glossopteris", "Mass extinction", "Punctuated equilibrium"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When an oil well is drilled in the Rio del Rey or Douala basins, the drill bit grinds the rock into small chips called cuttings. A trilobite or an ammonite would be smashed, but microscopic fossils survive whole. Micropalaeontologists study them to date the rocks and to say where the drill bit is. This lesson covers those tiny fossils, then the plants and vertebrates that record life on land."),
        H("Microfossils"),
        D("A **microfossil** is a fossil that must be studied with a microscope, usually smaller than about 1 mm. The study of fossil spores and pollen (and other organic-walled microfossils) is **palynology**.", title="Microfossil"),
        KEY("Main groups", "Wall material and range.", cards=[
            ["Foraminifera", "Single-celled protists with a chambered test, mostly calcareous. Planktonic forms such as *Globigerina* spread worldwide; large benthic forms such as *Nummulites* (Eocene) can build limestones. Cambrian to Recent."],
            ["Radiolaria and diatoms", "Siliceous skeletons. Radiolaria (protists, Cambrian to Recent) and diatoms (algae, Mesozoic to Recent) form siliceous oozes and cherts in deep water below the depth where calcite dissolves."],
            ["Coccoliths", "Tiny calcite plates of planktonic algae. They make up most of the Cretaceous **Chalk**. Jurassic to Recent."],
            ["Ostracods", "Small crustaceans with two valves. Different species live in marine, brackish and fresh water, so they are good salinity indicators."],
            ["Conodonts", "Tooth-like phosphatic elements of extinct eel-like animals, Cambrian to Triassic. Their colour changes with heating, so they record the temperature reached by the rock."],
            ["Spores and pollen", "Tough organic walls that survive in non-marine rocks and coals; carried by wind into every environment. Their colour also darkens with heating."],
        ]),
        KEY("Why the oil industry depends on microfossils", "Four reasons to learn.", cards=[
            ["Survive drilling", "They are small enough to be recovered whole from cuttings."],
            ["Abundant", "A few grams of rock can yield hundreds of specimens, enough for statistics."],
            ["Rapid evolution and wide spread", "Planktonic forms give fine biozones that correlate between wells."],
            ["Environment and heat", "Benthic forams and ostracods give water depth and salinity; spore and conodont colour show whether a source rock has reached the oil window."],
        ]),
        H("Plant fossils"),
        T("Land plants appear in the Silurian. By the **Carboniferous**, swamp forests of giant clubmosses and horsetails formed the coal seams of Europe and North America. Learn three of them: ***Lepidodendron*** (a tree-sized lycopod with diamond-shaped leaf scars in a spiral), ***Sigillaria*** (leaf scars in vertical rows) and ***Calamites*** (a jointed horsetail stem with vertical ribs). Their roots are called *Stigmaria*. Flowering plants (angiosperms) appear in the Cretaceous."),
        T("***Glossopteris***, a seed-fern with tongue-shaped leaves, is found in Permian rocks of South America, Africa, India, Antarctica and Australia. Its seeds could not cross oceans, so its distribution was among the early evidence that these continents were once joined in **Gondwana**. Plants are also climate indicators: growth rings suggest seasons, large leaves with smooth (entire) margins suggest warm wet climates, and thick cuticles with sunken stomata suggest dry ones."),
        H("Vertebrates"),
        KEY("Milestones in vertebrate history", "Approximate order; check exact ages in your textbook.", cards=[
            ["Fishes", "Jawless fishes in the Ordovician; jawed and bony fishes diversify in the Devonian, the 'age of fishes'."],
            ["Amphibians", "Late Devonian tetrapods evolved from lobe-finned fishes, still tied to water to breed."],
            ["Reptiles", "Carboniferous; the amniotic egg allowed life away from water. Dinosaurs ruled the Mesozoic (Triassic to Cretaceous)."],
            ["Birds and mammals", "Mammals from the Late Triassic; *Archaeopteryx* (Late Jurassic) shows both reptile and bird features, a transitional fossil. Mammals diversified after the dinosaurs died out."],
        ]),
        T("Vertebrate fossils are rarer than shells: bones are scattered by scavengers and land animals are seldom buried. They are therefore of little use for dating rocks, but they are very important for the story of evolution and for reconstructing land environments."),
        H("Evolution and extinction in the fossil record"),
        T("Darwin explained evolution by natural selection: variation, inheritance and differential survival. Two models describe its pace. **Phyletic gradualism** expects slow, continuous change within a lineage, with many intermediate forms (the *Micraster* series is often quoted). **Punctuated equilibrium**, proposed by Eldredge and Gould in 1972, expects long periods with little change (stasis) broken by rapid speciation in small isolated populations, so intermediates are rare. The fossil record contains examples of both."),
        T("**Mass extinctions** remove a large share of species in a geologically short time. The 'big five' are usually given as the end-Ordovician, Late Devonian, end-Permian (the largest, about 252 million years ago), end-Triassic and end-Cretaceous (about 66 million years ago, when the dinosaurs and ammonites disappeared; a meteorite impact is supported by an iridium-rich clay layer, an idea proposed by the Alvarez team in 1980). Extinctions make sharp, recognizable boundaries in the stratigraphic record."),
        EX("Worked example 1: choosing a fossil for a borehole", intro="A company drills a well through 2 000 m of Cretaceous and Tertiary marine shales. The geologist must date the rocks from cuttings. Which fossil group should she use and why?", steps=[
            S("Large fossils", text="Ammonites or bivalves are broken by the drill bit and are rare in small chips."),
            S("Microfossils", text="Planktonic foraminifera and coccoliths are abundant in marine shales, survive whole in cuttings and evolved quickly."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Use planktonic foraminifera (and nannofossils such as coccoliths), with spores and pollen for any non-marine beds."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: gradualism or punctuation?", intro="In a 50 m section, a bivalve lineage shows no change in shape for 45 m, then a new species appears suddenly with no intermediates in the next metre. Which model does it support, and what else could explain it?", steps=[
            S("Pattern", text="Long stasis followed by an abrupt change fits punctuated equilibrium."),
            S("Alternative", text="A gap in deposition (a non-sequence or unconformity) or migration of the new species from elsewhere could also produce a sudden change."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Supports punctuated equilibrium only if the section is shown to be continuous."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling conodonts or foraminifera 'small shells of animals'", "Foraminifera and radiolaria are single-celled protists; coccoliths and diatoms are algae; conodonts are parts of extinct vertebrate-like animals. Name the group correctly."),
        MISTAKE("Using vertebrates for zone dating", "Vertebrate fossils are rare and patchy, so they are not good zone fossils. Use them for evolution and environments."),
        QUIZ("Why are spores and pollen useful for correlating non-marine rocks such as coal measures?", ["They have siliceous skeletons", "They are blown into every environment and their tough organic walls resist decay", "They are found only in deep marine rocks", "They are large and easy to see in the field"], 1,
             "Wind dispersal spreads them widely; sporopollenin walls survive.", hint="How do spores travel?",
             feedback=["Spores and pollen have organic walls.", "", "They occur in all environments, including non-marine ones.", "They are microscopic."]),
        QUIZ("The distribution of Glossopteris in Permian rocks of several southern continents supports:", ["The end-Cretaceous impact", "The former union of the continents in Gondwana", "Punctuated equilibrium", "The evolution of flowering plants"], 1,
             "Its heavy seeds could not cross oceans, so the continents must have been joined.", hint="Where is it found?",
             feedback=["That event is much later.", "", "This is about distribution, not pace of evolution.", "Glossopteris was a seed fern, not a flowering plant."]),
        TIP("Paper 1 asks about groups of microfossils and plants; Paper 2 essays may ask about the value of microfossils to the oil industry, about mass extinctions or about the evidence for evolution. Give named examples and explain each point.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Explain why microfossils are more useful than macrofossils in petroleum exploration.",
           "They survive drilling and are recovered whole from cuttings; they are abundant, so small samples give many specimens; planktonic groups evolved fast and spread widely, giving fine zones for correlation between wells; benthic forms indicate water depth and salinity; the colour of spores, pollen and conodonts shows the thermal maturity of source rocks. Macrofossils are broken by the bit and are too rare in small samples."),
        PQ("Describe three Carboniferous plant fossils and explain how plant fossils can be used as climatic indicators.",
           "Lepidodendron: lycopod tree with spiral diamond-shaped leaf scars. Sigillaria: lycopod with leaf scars in vertical rows. Calamites: jointed horsetail stem with longitudinal ribs. Climate: growth rings indicate seasonal climates, their absence a uniform tropical climate; large entire-margined leaves suggest warm wet conditions; thick cuticles and sunken stomata suggest arid conditions; coal-swamp floras indicate warm humid swamps."),
        PQ("Plan an essay: 'Discuss the evidence from the fossil record for evolution.'",
           "Introduction: evolution is change in populations over time; fossils provide a time-ordered record. Points: faunal succession, with simple forms in older rocks and new groups appearing in order; evolutionary lineages such as graptolite stipe reduction, ammonoid suture complexity and Micraster; transitional fossils such as Archaeopteryx and Devonian lobe-finned fishes to tetrapods; mass extinctions followed by radiations. Evaluation: the record is incomplete (preservation potential, gaps), which affects the debate between phyletic gradualism and punctuated equilibrium (Eldredge and Gould). Conclusion: despite gaps, the order of fossils strongly supports evolution."),
        SUM("Microfossils (forams, radiolaria, diatoms, coccoliths, ostracods, conodonts, spores and pollen) date rocks from cuttings.",
            "Spore and conodont colour show how hot a rock has been, a guide to oil maturity.",
            "Lepidodendron, Sigillaria and Calamites built Carboniferous coal swamps; Glossopteris supports Gondwana.",
            "Vertebrate history runs from fishes to amphibians, reptiles, mammals and birds; vertebrates are poor zone fossils.",
            "The fossil record shows evolution, with gradual and punctuated patterns, and five major mass extinctions."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Principles of stratigraphy
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_STR,
    topic="Principles of stratigraphy",
    title="Lithostratigraphy, biostratigraphy, chronostratigraphy and radiometric dating",
    slug="gyu2-stratigraphy-principles",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Stratigraphy divides rocks in three ways: by rock type (lithostratigraphy), by fossil content (biostratigraphy) and by time (chronostratigraphy). Biozones from range charts correlate rocks, unconformities mark gaps, and radiometric dating converts relative order into ages in years.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish lithostratigraphic, biostratigraphic and chronostratigraphic units and the geochronological units of time",
        "Define range, concurrent-range, assemblage and acme zones and read them from a range chart",
        "Apply Walther's law and recognize unconformities, transgressions and regressions",
        "Calculate radiometric ages from half-lives and daughter-to-parent ratios and describe U-Pb, K-Ar and Rb-Sr dating",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Formation", "Biozone", "Range chart", "Chronostratigraphy", "GSSP", "Walther's law", "Unconformity", "Half-life", "Radiometric dating"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("At O Level you used superposition, cross-cutting relationships and faunal succession to put rocks in order. At A Level you must name and define the units that stratigraphers use, correlate rocks with fossil zones and give ages in millions of years. A borehole in the Douala basin and a cliff in Europe can only be compared if both are described in the same language. That language is the subject of this lesson."),
        H("Three ways to divide rocks"),
        KEY("Kinds of stratigraphic unit", "Each is defined by different evidence.", cards=[
            ["Lithostratigraphy", "Units defined by **rock type**, regardless of age: group, **formation**, member, bed. A formation is the basic mappable unit with a type section and a name (often a place name). Its boundaries may cut across time lines (be diachronous)."],
            ["Biostratigraphy", "Units defined by **fossil content**: the **biozone**. A zone is named after a characteristic fossil."],
            ["Chronostratigraphy", "Units of **rock** formed during a given span of time: eonothem, erathem, system, series, stage. Matching units of **time** (geochronology) are eon, era, period, epoch, age. For example, the Jurassic **System** is the rock formed during the Jurassic **Period**."],
        ]),
        T("Chronostratigraphic boundaries are fixed internationally at a chosen point in a chosen rock section, the **Global Boundary Stratotype Section and Point (GSSP)**, informally a 'golden spike'. Most boundaries are placed at the first appearance of a particular fossil, so biostratigraphy is the main tool for recognizing them."),
        H("Biozones and range charts"),
        D("A **range zone** is the body of rock containing a particular taxon, from its first to its last appearance. A **concurrent-range zone** is the overlap of the ranges of two taxa. An **assemblage zone** is characterized by a group of fossils occurring together. An **acme zone** is where a taxon is most abundant.", title="Kinds of biozone"),
        IMG("algyu2-rangechart.svg", alt="Range chart: a column of beds numbered 1 at the bottom to 10 at the top, with vertical bars for five fossil species A to E. A ranges from bed 1 to 6, B from 4 to 9, C from 2 to 4, D from 7 to 10 and E from 5 to 7",
            caption="Figure 1. A range chart for five fossil species (teaching data). Beds are numbered from the oldest (1) to the youngest (10)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reading a range chart", intro="Using Figure 1, (a) give the concurrent-range zone of A and B; (b) a sample contains B and E but not A. Which beds could it come from?", steps=[
            S("Overlap of A and B", text="A ranges from 1 to 6 and B from 4 to 9, so the concurrent-range zone is beds 4 to 6."),
            S("B and E together", text="B: 4 to 9; E: 5 to 7. Both present in beds 5 to 7."),
            S("Remove beds with A", text="A is present up to bed 6, so beds 5 and 6 would also contain A (if it was preserved). The sample most probably comes from bed 7."),
        ]),
        T("The ideal zone fossil evolves rapidly (short range), is widespread (planktonic or nektonic), is abundant, is easy to identify, lives in many facies and is well preserved. Graptolites, ammonites and planktonic foraminifera come closest. Zone boundaries can be affected by migration, facies control and reworking of older fossils into younger rocks."),
        H("Facies, Walther's law and sea-level change"),
        D("A **facies** is a body of rock with characteristics (lithology, structures, fossils) that reflect its environment of deposition. **Walther's law** states that, in a conformable succession, facies that are found on top of each other were deposited in environments that lay side by side.", title="Facies and Walther's law"),
        T("When the sea rises over the land (**transgression**), offshore facies are deposited over nearshore facies, giving a **fining-upward** and deepening sequence: for example sandstone, then shale, then limestone. When the sea falls (**regression**), the sequence coarsens upward. Facies boundaries then cut across time lines, so a formation can be younger in one place than another."),
        KEY("Unconformities", "Gaps in the record.", cards=[
            ["Angular unconformity", "Younger beds rest on older beds that were tilted or folded and eroded."],
            ["Disconformity", "Parallel beds above and below, but an erosion surface between them."],
            ["Nonconformity", "Sedimentary rocks rest on eroded igneous or metamorphic rocks, as where Cretaceous sediments rest on the Precambrian basement in Cameroon."],
            ["Paraconformity", "Parallel beds with no visible erosion surface; the gap is shown only by missing fossil zones."],
        ]),
        H("Radiometric dating"),
        D("**Radiometric dating** is a method of absolute dating that gives the age of a rock or mineral in years from the decay of radioactive isotopes. The **half-life** is the time for half of the parent atoms in a sample to decay to daughter atoms.", title="Radiometric dating"),
        F(r"t = \frac{t_{1/2}}{\ln 2}\,\ln\!\left(1 + \frac{D}{P}\right)", title="Age from the daughter-to-parent ratio"),
        T("Here $D$ is the number of daughter atoms produced by decay and $P$ the number of parent atoms left. After $n$ half-lives the fraction of parent remaining is $(1/2)^n$, so if $D/P = 1$, one half-life has passed; if $D/P = 3$, two half-lives; if $D/P = 7$, three."),
        KEY("Common methods", "Half-lives as usually quoted; check your textbook for the values it uses.", cards=[
            ["Uranium-lead", "U-238 decays to Pb-206 (half-life about 4 470 million years) and U-235 to Pb-207 (about 704 million years). Used on zircon in granites and gneisses; two decay chains cross-check each other."],
            ["Potassium-argon", "K-40 decays mainly to Ca-40 and partly (about 11%) to Ar-40; overall half-life about 1 250 million years. Only the argon branch is useful, because calcium is too common. Argon is a gas and leaks out on heating, so ages can be too young."],
            ["Rubidium-strontium", "Rb-87 decays to Sr-87 with a very long half-life (about 48 800 million years quoted in many textbooks). Used on micas and potassium feldspars of old granites and metamorphic rocks."],
            ["Carbon-14", "Half-life about 5 730 years; only for organic material younger than about 50 000 years, so of little use for rocks."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a U-Pb age", intro="A zircon from a granite has a Pb-206 to U-238 ratio of 0.25 (assume all the lead is radiogenic). The half-life of U-238 is 4 470 million years. Calculate its age.", steps=[
            S("Substitute", math=r"t = \frac{4\,470}{0.693}\,\ln(1.25)"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"t = 6\,450 \times 0.2231 \approx 1\,440 \text{ million years}"),
            S("Interpret", text="About 1.44 billion years, a Mesoproterozoic crystallization age for the granite."),
        ], check=r"\left(\tfrac12\right)^{1440/4470} \approx 0.80 = \frac{1}{1.25}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: half-lives", intro="A mineral contains 12.5% of its original parent isotope. The half-life is 704 million years. How old is it?", steps=[
            S("Number of half-lives", math=r"\left(\tfrac12\right)^n = 0.125 \Rightarrow n = 3"),
            S("Age", math=r"3 \times 704 = 2\,112 \text{ million years}"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing time and rock units", "The Cretaceous **Period** is time; the Cretaceous **System** is rock. Write 'rocks of the Cretaceous System' or 'during the Cretaceous Period', not 'the Cretaceous System lasted 79 million years'."),
        MISTAKE("Dating sedimentary rocks directly", "Radiometric ages of detrital grains give the age of the source rock, not of deposition. Date sediments by bracketing them between dated lavas, ash beds or intrusions, or by glauconite."),
        QUIZ("A mineral has a daughter-to-parent ratio of 3. How many half-lives have passed?", ["1", "1.5", "2", "3"], 2,
             "If D/P = 3, the parent is 1/4 of the original: two half-lives.", hint="What fraction of the original parent is left?",
             feedback=["D/P = 1 after one half-life.", "There is no simple ratio of 3 at 1.5 half-lives.", "", "After three half-lives D/P = 7."]),
        QUIZ("Which is a chronostratigraphic unit?", ["The Jurassic System", "The Jurassic Period", "A sandstone formation", "An ammonite zone"], 0,
             "System, series and stage are chronostratigraphic (rock) units.", hint="Rock formed during a time span.",
             feedback=["", "A period is a unit of time (geochronology).", "A formation is lithostratigraphic.", "A zone is biostratigraphic."]),
        TIP("Paper 1 tests unit names, zone types and half-life calculations. The Board's examiners reported that few candidates could define radiometric dating or describe two methods for dating granites: learn the U-Pb, K-Ar and Rb-Sr methods with their parent, daughter and limitations.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Define radiometric dating and describe two radiometric methods that could be used to date a granite.",
           "Radiometric dating gives the absolute age of a rock in years from the decay of radioactive isotopes of known half-life. U-Pb: U-238 to Pb-206 (half-life about 4 470 Ma) and U-235 to Pb-207 (about 704 Ma) measured in zircon; two chains give a check; zircon resists alteration. Rb-Sr: Rb-87 to Sr-87 (very long half-life) in micas and K-feldspar; several minerals plotted on an isochron give the age and the initial Sr ratio. (K-Ar: K-40 to Ar-40 in micas, but argon loss on reheating gives young ages.)"),
        PQ("A biotite has a Sr-87 (radiogenic) to Rb-87 ratio of 0.01. Using a half-life of 48 800 million years, calculate its age.",
           r"t = (48 800 ÷ 0.693) × ln(1.01) = 70 420 × 0.00995 ≈ 700 million years."),
        PQ("Explain Walther's law and how it can be used to recognize a marine transgression in a vertical section.",
           "In a conformable succession, facies stacked vertically were deposited in laterally adjacent environments. In a transgression the shoreline moves landward, so deeper-water facies come to lie on shallower ones: for example beach sandstone, then offshore shale, then shelf limestone, fining and deepening upwards. A basal conglomerate may rest on an unconformity at the base."),
        SUM("Lithostratigraphy (formations), biostratigraphy (zones) and chronostratigraphy (systems, stages) divide rocks in different ways.",
            "Time units (period, epoch, age) match rock units (system, series, stage); boundaries are fixed at GSSPs.",
            "Range, concurrent-range, assemblage and acme zones are read from range charts.",
            "Walther's law links vertical facies changes to transgressions and regressions; unconformities mark gaps.",
            r"Radiometric age: $t = (t_{1/2}/\ln 2)\ln(1 + D/P)$; U-Pb, K-Ar and Rb-Sr date igneous and metamorphic rocks."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. The Precambrian basement of Cameroon
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_STR,
    topic="The Precambrian basement of Cameroon",
    title="The Precambrian basement: the Congo Craton, the Ntem Complex and the Nyong unit",
    slug="gyu2-precambrian-basement-cameroon",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Most of Cameroon is underlain by Precambrian crystalline rocks. In the south lies the Archaean Ntem Complex, part of the Congo Craton, made of charnockites, TTG gneisses and greenstone belts with banded iron formations; at its north-western edge the Nyong unit was reworked in the Palaeoproterozoic.",
    objectives=[
        "Define craton, shield, mobile belt, TTG gneiss, charnockite, greenstone belt and banded iron formation",
        "Describe the rocks of the Ntem Complex and explain what they show about the early crust",
        "Describe the Nyong unit as Palaeoproterozoic reworking of the craton margin",
        "Relate the Precambrian basement of Cameroon to its iron ore and to the supercontinent story",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Craton", "Shield", "Archaean", "TTG gneiss", "Charnockite", "Greenstone belt", "Banded iron formation", "Eburnean orogeny"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Drive from Yaoundé south towards Ebolowa and Ambam and you cross some of the oldest rocks in Africa. Most of Cameroon is underlain by crystalline **basement**: granites, gneisses, schists and charnockites formed in the Precambrian, more than 541 million years ago. Younger sediments and volcanic rocks cover only parts of the country. This lesson looks at the oldest part of that basement in the south."),
        T("Exact names, boundaries and ages of Cameroonian units vary between textbooks and research papers. The names below are those commonly used. Treat every age as approximate and check your textbook."),
        H("Key terms"),
        D("A **craton** is an old, thick, stable part of a continent that has not been strongly deformed for a very long time (in Africa, since the Precambrian). Where it is exposed at the surface it is a **shield**. A **mobile belt** (orogenic belt) is a zone of younger deformation, metamorphism and granite intrusion, often around a craton.", title="Craton, shield and mobile belt"),
        D("**TTG gneisses** are tonalite, trondhjemite and granodiorite gneisses, sodium-rich rocks typical of the Archaean continental crust. **Charnockites** are orthopyroxene-bearing granitic rocks formed at high temperature in the granulite facies. A **greenstone belt** is a belt of metamorphosed volcanic rocks (often basaltic and ultramafic) and sediments, low-grade and green from chlorite and epidote. A **banded iron formation (BIF)** is a chemical sediment of alternating iron-oxide-rich and silica-rich (chert) bands.", title="Rock types of Archaean terrains"),
        H("The Congo Craton and the Ntem Complex"),
        T("Southern Cameroon lies on the north-western part of the **Congo Craton**, which continues into Gabon, Equatorial Guinea and the Congo. Its Cameroonian part is called the **Ntem Complex** (after the Ntem River). It is **Archaean**, with ages commonly given around 3.0 to 2.6 billion years: check your textbook for the figures it uses."),
        KEY("Rocks of the Ntem Complex", "Typical of Archaean cratons worldwide.", cards=[
            ["Charnockites and TTG gneisses", "Form most of the complex: old continental crust metamorphosed at high temperature."],
            ["Greenstone belts", "Remnants of volcanic and sedimentary belts, metamorphosed and folded between the gneisses."],
            ["Banded iron formations", "Iron-rich chemical sediments in the greenstone belts, the source of large iron ore prospects in the south and south-east, such as the deposit commonly cited at Mbalam (check your textbook)."],
            ["Late granites and dykes", "Potassic granites intruded the gneisses near the end of the Archaean; later dolerite dykes cut the craton."],
        ]),
        T("These rocks tell us about the early Earth. TTG gneisses formed by partial melting of hydrated basaltic crust, probably at higher temperatures than today. BIFs show that iron dissolved in the oceans, which is possible only when the oceans and atmosphere had little free oxygen; most BIFs are older than about 1.8 billion years."),
        H("The Nyong unit: reworking at the edge of the craton"),
        T("North-west of the Ntem Complex lies the **Nyong unit** (also called the Nyong Series, Group or Complex). It consists of Archaean rocks that were deformed and metamorphosed again, together with Palaeoproterozoic sediments and igneous rocks, during the **Eburnean orogeny** around 2.1 to 2.0 billion years ago. Some studies describe high-pressure rocks such as eclogites in it. It is often correlated with similar rocks of the São Luís craton in north-east Brazil, which was next to Cameroon before the South Atlantic opened. Check your textbook for the name and age it uses."),
        CODE("""
Approximate age (billion years)  Event in the basement of southern Cameroon
-------------------------------  ---------------------------------------------
about 3.0 to 2.6                 Ntem Complex: TTG gneisses, charnockites,
                                 greenstone belts with BIF; late K-granites
about 2.1 to 2.0                 Eburnean orogeny: Nyong unit reworked at
                                 the north-west margin of the Congo Craton
about 0.65 to 0.55               Pan-African orogeny to the north: nappes
                                 thrust southward onto the craton edge
Ages are approximate teaching values: check your textbook.
""", "text", title="Table 1. Outline history of the southern basement"),
        H("Why the basement matters"),
        T("The basement forms most of the land surface of Cameroon. It weathers deeply under the tropical climate to give thick lateritic soils, aquifers in the weathered and fractured rock, and residual deposits of iron, aluminium and other metals. Its BIFs are among the most important iron prospects of Central Africa. The craton also acted as a rigid block against which the younger Pan-African belt was squeezed (next lesson)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: identifying an Archaean terrain", intro="A field party in southern Cameroon maps coarse gneisses rich in sodic plagioclase, dark orthopyroxene-bearing granitic rocks and a narrow belt of chlorite schists with layers of magnetite and chert. Identify each rock type and the terrain.", steps=[
            S("Sodic plagioclase gneisses", text="TTG gneisses."),
            S("Orthopyroxene-bearing granitic rocks", text="Charnockites, formed in the granulite facies."),
            S("Chlorite schists with magnetite-chert layers", text="A greenstone belt with a banded iron formation."),
            S("Conclusion", text="An Archaean craton terrain: the Ntem Complex of the Congo Craton."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: an essay plan", intro="Plan an answer: 'Describe the geology of the Precambrian basement of southern Cameroon.'", steps=[
            S("Introduction", text="Define basement and craton; locate the Ntem Complex on the north-west Congo Craton."),
            S("Ntem Complex", text="TTG gneisses and charnockites; greenstone belts with BIF; late potassic granites; Archaean age (hedged)."),
            S("Nyong unit", text="Palaeoproterozoic (Eburnean) reworking at the north-west margin; link with Brazil before the Atlantic opened."),
            S("Significance", text="Iron ore in BIFs, deep weathering and laterites, groundwater; rigid foreland for the Pan-African belt."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Giving precise ages as facts", "Ages of Cameroonian units differ between studies. Write 'about 3 billion years (Archaean)' and name the era correctly rather than quoting an exact figure you are not sure of."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing charnockite with granite", "A charnockite has granitic composition but contains orthopyroxene (hypersthene) and formed at granulite-facies temperatures. Name the mineral that makes the difference."),
        QUIZ("Which rock is a chemical sediment typical of Archaean greenstone belts and an important iron ore source?", ["Charnockite", "Banded iron formation", "Trondhjemite", "Dolerite"], 1,
             "BIF is alternating iron oxide and chert bands.", hint="Which one is a sediment?",
             feedback=["Charnockite is a high-grade granitic rock.", "", "Trondhjemite is a sodic granitic rock of TTG suites.", "Dolerite is a medium-grained basic igneous rock in dykes."]),
        QUIZ("The Ntem Complex is best described as:", ["A Cretaceous sedimentary basin", "The Archaean north-western part of the Congo Craton", "A Pan-African fold belt", "A chain of Cenozoic volcanoes"], 1,
             "It is the Archaean craton of southern Cameroon.", hint="How old is it?",
             feedback=["The coastal and inland basins are Cretaceous.", "", "The Pan-African belt lies to the north.", "That is the Cameroon Volcanic Line."]),
        TIP("Questions on the geology of Cameroon can appear in Paper 1 and Paper 2. Examiners reward correct terms (craton, TTG, greenstone belt, BIF), correct order of events and sensible hedging of ages.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Define craton and explain why the Ntem Complex is regarded as part of a craton.",
           "A craton is an old, thick, stable part of a continent not strongly deformed for a very long time. The Ntem Complex is made of Archaean gneisses, charnockites and greenstone belts that were stabilized by the end of the Archaean and were not strongly deformed by the later Pan-African orogeny, which affected the rocks to the north instead."),
        PQ("Explain how banded iron formations formed and why they are found mainly in Precambrian rocks.",
           "Iron dissolved as Fe2+ in oxygen-poor oceans. Where it met oxygen (for example from photosynthesizing microbes) it was oxidized and precipitated as iron oxides, alternating with silica (chert) layers, perhaps seasonally. Once the atmosphere and oceans contained abundant oxygen (after about 1.8 billion years ago) iron could no longer stay dissolved in seawater, so BIFs became rare."),
        PQ("Describe the Nyong unit and its significance.",
           "The Nyong unit lies at the north-west margin of the Ntem Complex. It contains Archaean rocks reworked, with Palaeoproterozoic rocks, during the Eburnean orogeny (about 2 billion years ago; check your textbook). It shows that the margin of the craton was involved in an early mountain-building event, and its similarity to rocks in north-east Brazil supports the reconstruction of Africa and South America before the Atlantic opened."),
        SUM("Most of Cameroon is underlain by Precambrian crystalline basement.",
            "The Ntem Complex is the Archaean north-western Congo Craton: TTG gneisses, charnockites, greenstone belts with BIF, late granites.",
            "The Nyong unit at the north-west margin was reworked in the Eburnean orogeny (about 2 billion years ago).",
            "BIFs record oxygen-poor early oceans and are the main iron ore prospects of the south.",
            "Ages and names vary between sources: hedge them and check your textbook."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. The Pan-African fold belt
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_STR,
    topic="The Pan-African fold belt",
    title="The Pan-African Central African Fold Belt in Cameroon",
    slug="gyu2-pan-african-fold-belt",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Late in the Neoproterozoic, collision during the assembly of Gondwana built the Pan-African Central African Fold Belt across Cameroon. Nappes were thrust south onto the Congo Craton, rocks were metamorphosed and intruded by granites, and shear zones such as the Central Cameroon Shear Zone formed.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain the Pan-African orogeny as continental collision during the assembly of Gondwana",
        "Describe the main domains of the fold belt in Cameroon and the Yaoundé nappes",
        "Explain the formation and features of a major shear zone, using the Central Cameroon Shear Zone",
        "Relate syn- and post-tectonic granites and gold mineralization to the orogeny",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Orogeny", "Pan-African", "Gondwana", "Nappe", "Granulite facies", "Shear zone", "Mylonite", "Syn-tectonic granite"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The granites of the Adamawa plateau, the gneisses around Yaoundé and the schists of the north were not formed in the Archaean. Most of them were made or remade in a mountain-building event between roughly 650 and 550 million years ago, called the **Pan-African orogeny**. The mountains have long been eroded, but their roots remain: deformed and metamorphosed rocks, granites and huge shear zones."),
        D("An **orogeny** is a mountain-building event involving folding, thrusting, metamorphism and igneous activity, usually at a convergent plate boundary. The **Pan-African orogeny** is the name for the group of Neoproterozoic to Early Cambrian orogenies (roughly 650 to 550 million years ago) by which several continental blocks collided to form the supercontinent **Gondwana**.", title="Orogeny and the Pan-African"),
        T("In Cameroon the belt is called the **Central African Fold Belt** (or the North Equatorial Pan-African belt; parts are also called the Oubanguides). It formed between the **Congo Craton** to the south and the **West African Craton** and the Saharan block (often called the Saharan metacraton) to the north. Before the South Atlantic opened, the belt continued into the Borborema Province of north-east Brazil."),
        IMG("algyu2-panafrican.svg", alt="Schematic south to north section across Cameroon: the Congo Craton in the south, the Yaoundé domain with nappes thrust southward onto the craton, the Adamawa-Yadé domain, the Central Cameroon Shear Zone shown as a vertical zone, and the North-West Cameroon domain",
            caption="Figure 1. Schematic south-north section of the Pan-African belt in Cameroon (not to scale; names and limits vary between sources)."),
        KEY("Domains of the fold belt", "A common three-fold division; check your textbook's version.", cards=[
            ["Southern (Yaoundé) domain", "Metasediments of the **Yaoundé Group** metamorphosed at high pressure and high temperature (garnet-kyanite gneisses, granulites), then thrust southward as **nappes** onto the Congo Craton."],
            ["Central (Adamawa-Yadé) domain", "Older (Palaeoproterozoic) crust reworked in the Pan-African, intruded by many granitoids and cut by large shear zones."],
            ["North-western domain", "Volcanic and sedimentary rocks metamorphosed at lower grade (for example the Poli area), with arc-type granitoids, interpreted by many authors as a former magmatic arc."],
        ]),
        H("Nappes against the craton"),
        D("A **nappe** is a large sheet of rock moved a great distance (kilometres to tens of kilometres) over a low-angle thrust fault, or forming the overturned limb of a recumbent fold.", title="Nappe"),
        T("At the southern edge of the belt, rocks of the Yaoundé domain were pushed south over the rigid craton. Their mineral assemblages (garnet, kyanite, in places granulite-facies assemblages) show burial to great depth during collision, followed by uplift and erosion. This is the classic pattern of a collisional orogen: a stiff craton acts as the foreland, and the mobile belt is thrust over it."),
        H("Shear zones"),
        D("A **shear zone** is a tabular zone of intense deformation in which rocks on either side have moved past each other. Deep in the crust, at high temperature, the deformation is ductile and produces **mylonites**: fine-grained, strongly foliated and lineated rocks formed by grain-size reduction.", title="Shear zone"),
        T("The **Central Cameroon Shear Zone** (CCSZ) is a major, roughly ENE-WSW (about N70°E) trending, mainly **dextral** (right-lateral) strike-slip shear zone crossing the country. Similar shear zones, such as the Sanaga fault, cut the belt. The CCSZ is usually correlated with the Pernambuco shear zone of Brazil. Later, these old zones of weakness were reactivated: Cretaceous basins formed along some of them, and the Cameroon Volcanic Line may follow them (check your textbook)."),
        KEY("Granites of the orogeny", "Classified by their timing relative to deformation.", cards=[
            ["Syn-tectonic", "Intruded during deformation, so they are foliated parallel to the regional fabric."],
            ["Late- to post-tectonic", "Intruded after the main deformation, so they cut the fabric and are not foliated. Many plutons of the Adamawa and the west are of this type."],
        ]),
        T("Gold in eastern Cameroon (for example the areas commonly cited around Bétaré-Oya and Batouri) occurs in quartz veins and in placers derived from them. Many studies link the veins to fluids that moved along Pan-African shear zones; check your textbook."),
        EX("Worked example 1: syn- or post-tectonic?", intro="Granite A has aligned feldspar crystals parallel to the foliation of the surrounding gneiss. Granite B cuts across the gneiss foliation and contains xenoliths of foliated gneiss, but is itself unfoliated. Interpret both.", steps=[
            S("Granite A", text="Its foliation matches the regional fabric, so it was intruded during deformation: syn-tectonic."),
            S("Granite B", text="It cuts the foliation and contains foliated xenoliths, so it is younger than the deformation (cross-cutting and inclusions)."),
            S("Conclusion", text="B is post-tectonic. Together they bracket the age of the main deformation, which could be dated by U-Pb on zircon from each granite."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: dating the collision", intro="Zircon from a syn-tectonic granite gives 615 million years. Zircon from a post-tectonic granite cutting the same fabric gives 575 million years (teaching values). What can you say about the deformation?", steps=[
            S("Syn-tectonic age", text="Deformation was taking place at about 615 million years ago."),
            S("Post-tectonic age", text="Deformation had ended before 575 million years ago."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The main deformation falls between about 615 and 575 million years ago: Pan-African (Neoproterozoic)."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling the Pan-African belt a volcanic line", "The Pan-African belt is a Neoproterozoic orogenic belt of deformed, metamorphosed and intruded rocks. The Cameroon Volcanic Line is Cenozoic volcanism that partly follows its old structures."),
        MISTAKE("Describing a shear zone as a single fault plane", "A ductile shear zone is a zone, often kilometres wide, of mylonites and strong fabric, not one brittle break."),
        QUIZ("Which supercontinent was assembled during the Pan-African orogeny?", ["Pangaea", "Gondwana", "Rodinia", "Laurasia"], 1,
             "The Pan-African collisions assembled Gondwana at the end of the Neoproterozoic.", hint="Which supercontinent included Africa and South America in the late Precambrian?",
             feedback=["Pangaea formed later, in the late Palaeozoic.", "", "Rodinia broke up before the Pan-African.", "Laurasia is the northern half of Pangaea."]),
        QUIZ("What rock forms in the core of a ductile shear zone?", ["Breccia", "Mylonite", "Hornfels", "Conglomerate"], 1,
             "Mylonite is a fine-grained, foliated fault rock formed by ductile grain-size reduction.", hint="Ductile, not brittle.",
             feedback=["Breccia forms in brittle faults near the surface.", "", "Hornfels forms by contact metamorphism.", "Conglomerate is a sedimentary rock."]),
        TIP("Paper 2 may ask you to describe the Pan-African belt of Cameroon or to explain the formation of a shear zone; Paper 1 tests the terms. Use a labelled sketch section (craton, nappes, domains, shear zone) and hedge ages.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe the main features of the Pan-African fold belt in Cameroon.",
           "Formed about 650 to 550 million years ago (check your textbook) by collision between the Congo Craton and blocks to the north during the assembly of Gondwana. Southern domain: Yaoundé Group metasediments at high pressure, thrust south as nappes onto the craton. Central domain: reworked older crust, many granitoids, shear zones. North-western domain: lower-grade volcano-sedimentary rocks and arc granitoids. Major dextral shear zones (the Central Cameroon Shear Zone, Sanaga fault). Syn- and post-tectonic granites. Continuation into the Borborema Province of Brazil."),
        PQ("Explain how a ductile shear zone forms and how mylonites can be recognized.",
           "Where two crustal blocks move past each other at depth, high temperature and pressure make rocks deform by ductile flow rather than fracture. Strain is concentrated in a zone where grains are reduced in size by dynamic recrystallization. Mylonites are fine-grained, strongly foliated and lineated, often with larger rounded relict grains (porphyroclasts) whose tails show the sense of shear."),
        PQ("Explain why the Congo Craton was not strongly deformed during the Pan-African orogeny while the rocks to its north were.",
           "The craton is old, cold, thick and strong, with a deep lithospheric root, so it resisted deformation and acted as a rigid foreland. The younger, hotter and weaker rocks to the north were squeezed, buried, metamorphosed and intruded, and were thrust over the craton edge as nappes."),
        SUM("The Pan-African orogeny (about 650 to 550 million years ago) assembled Gondwana.",
            "In Cameroon it built the Central African Fold Belt between the Congo Craton and blocks to the north.",
            "Yaoundé Group nappes were metamorphosed at high pressure and thrust south onto the craton.",
            "Dextral shear zones such as the Central Cameroon Shear Zone cross the belt and continue into Brazil.",
            "Syn- and post-tectonic granites and shear-zone-related gold are products of the orogeny."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10. Sedimentary basins of Cameroon
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_STR,
    topic="Sedimentary basins of Cameroon",
    title="Cretaceous sedimentary basins of Cameroon: rifts and passive margins",
    slug="gyu2-sedimentary-basins-cameroon",
    minutes=25,
    summary="The Cretaceous break-up of Gondwana opened the South Atlantic and rifts across Africa. In Cameroon it created the coastal Rio del Rey and Douala/Kribi-Campo basins and the inland Mamfe and Garoua basins, whose syn-rift and post-rift fills host the country's oil and gas.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain how the Cretaceous break-up of Gondwana created rift and passive-margin basins",
        "Name and locate the main Cretaceous basins of Cameroon and describe their fills",
        "Distinguish syn-rift and post-rift sequences and interpret a basin cross-section",
        "Calculate average sedimentation rates and relate basin history to petroleum",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Sedimentary basin", "Rift basin", "Passive margin", "Syn-rift", "Post-rift", "Benue Trough", "Rio del Rey basin", "Douala/Kribi-Campo basin", "Subsidence"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Cameroon's oil comes from rocks that are much younger than the basement: Cretaceous and Cenozoic sands and shales lying in basins near the coast. Inland, at Mamfe and Garoua, similar Cretaceous sandstones fill long, narrow troughs. All these basins were born at the same time, when Africa and South America began to separate. This lesson explains how."),
        D("A **sedimentary basin** is an area of the crust that subsided over a long period, so that a thick pile of sediment accumulated. A **rift basin** forms where the lithosphere is stretched and normal faults create a down-dropped trough (graben or half-graben). A **passive margin** is a continental margin with no plate boundary, formed after rifting has succeeded in opening an ocean; it subsides slowly as the stretched lithosphere cools.", title="Basin types"),
        H("The Cretaceous break-up"),
        T("In the Early Cretaceous, Gondwana began to break apart. The **South Atlantic** opened between Africa and South America, and a system of rifts, the **West and Central African Rift System**, cut across the continent. Its best-known part is the **Benue Trough** of Nigeria, which branches north-east into Cameroon. Many rifts followed the old Pan-African shear zones, which were weak. The Gulf of Guinea margin formed partly by transform (strike-slip) movement along major oceanic fracture zones."),
        IMG("algyu2-basins.svg", alt="Cross-section of a rift basin: tilted fault blocks bounded by normal faults with wedge-shaped syn-rift continental sandstones between them, overlain by flat-lying post-rift marine shales and sands that thicken towards the centre, all resting on crystalline basement",
            caption="Figure 1. Schematic rift basin: syn-rift wedges in half-grabens, overlain by a post-rift (sag) sequence. Not to scale."),
        KEY("Two stages of basin fill", "Recognize them on a cross-section.", cards=[
            ["Syn-rift", "Deposited while the faults were active. Wedge-shaped units that thicken towards the faults. Often continental: alluvial fans and conglomerates at fault scarps, river sandstones, lake shales; sometimes evaporites when the sea first enters."],
            ["Post-rift (sag)", "Deposited after faulting stopped, as the lithosphere cooled and subsided. Broad, layered units that drape the faults and thicken towards the basin centre. Usually marine: shales, sandstones, limestones, and deltaic deposits."],
        ]),
        H("The basins of Cameroon"),
        T("The names and limits below are those commonly used; check your textbook, which may group them differently."),
        KEY("Main Cretaceous basins", "Coastal and inland.", cards=[
            ["Rio del Rey basin", "In the far west of the coast, next to Nigeria; the eastern edge of the Niger Delta system. Thick Cenozoic deltaic sands and shales over Cretaceous rocks. It has produced most of Cameroon's oil."],
            ["Douala/Kribi-Campo basin", "Along the coast from the Cameroon Volcanic Line south towards Equatorial Guinea; the Kribi-Campo part is narrow and the Douala part wider. Cretaceous to Cenozoic marine and deltaic sediments with oil and gas fields."],
            ["Mamfe basin", "An inland Cretaceous rift in the South-West, linked to the Benue Trough; mainly continental sandstones, conglomerates and shales resting on basement."],
            ["Garoua basin", "In the North, part of the Upper Benue rift system; Cretaceous continental sandstones and conglomerates in a half-graben."],
            ["Lake Chad basin", "In the Far North: Cretaceous and Cenozoic sediments, including the recent lake deposits of Lake Chad."],
        ]),
        T("The coastal basins have the full history: syn-rift continental sediments, then a transitional stage as sea water entered (with evaporites in some Gulf of Guinea basins), then a thick post-rift marine and deltaic sequence. The inland rifts never became oceans: they **failed** and contain mostly continental syn-rift fill."),
        H("Basins and petroleum"),
        T("A passive-margin basin can contain every element of a petroleum system: organic-rich shales (source), deltaic and turbidite sands (reservoirs), shales (seals) and faults and folds (traps). Burial to 2 to 4 km heats the source rock into the oil window. This is why the coastal basins, not the thin inland rifts, produce most oil (see the petroleum geology lesson)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a sedimentation rate", intro="A well in a coastal basin passes through 3 000 m of post-rift sediments deposited over about 60 million years (invented teaching figures). Calculate the average sedimentation rate in metres per million years and in millimetres per year.", steps=[
            S("Metres per million years", math=r"3\,000 \div 60 = 50 \text{ m per million years}"),
            S("Convert", math=r"50 \text{ m} = 50\,000 \text{ mm}, \quad 50\,000 \div 1\,000\,000 = 0.05 \text{ mm per year}"),
            S("Comment", text="This is an average of compacted thickness; real rates varied, and compaction makes the original sediment thicker."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: reading a basin section", intro="A section shows wedges of red conglomerate and sandstone thickening towards normal faults, overlain by parallel grey marine shales that cover the faults without being cut by them. Interpret the history.", steps=[
            S("Lower unit", text="Wedges thickening into active faults: syn-rift continental deposits (alluvial fans and rivers)."),
            S("Upper unit", text="Parallel marine shales draping the faults: post-rift deposits after faulting stopped."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Rifting with continental sedimentation, then thermal subsidence and a marine transgression: the pattern of the coastal basins."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying the basins formed in the Precambrian", "The basins are Cretaceous and younger; they rest unconformably (a nonconformity) on Precambrian basement."),
        MISTAKE("Calling every basin an oil basin", "Only some basins have mature source rocks, reservoirs, seals and traps. The coastal basins produce oil; the inland rifts are mostly continental and much less prospective."),
        QUIZ("Which basin, next to Nigeria, has produced most of Cameroon's oil?", ["Garoua basin", "Mamfe basin", "Rio del Rey basin", "Lake Chad basin"], 2,
             "The Rio del Rey basin is the eastern edge of the Niger Delta system.", hint="It is on the coast in the far west.",
             feedback=["Garoua is an inland rift in the North.", "Mamfe is an inland continental rift.", "", "The Lake Chad basin is in the Far North."]),
        QUIZ("Wedge-shaped units that thicken towards normal faults are:", ["Post-rift sediments", "Syn-rift sediments", "Basement rocks", "Volcanic rocks of the CVL"], 1,
             "Thickening into faults shows deposition while the faults were moving.", hint="When were the faults active?",
             feedback=["Post-rift units drape the faults with even thickness.", "", "Basement is crystalline and lies below the basin.", "Volcanic rocks are not wedge-shaped basin fills."]),
        TIP("Paper 2 may ask you to describe the sedimentary basins of Cameroon or explain their origin; Paper 1 may ask you to match a basin to its setting. Draw a labelled sketch section and link the basins to the opening of the South Atlantic and the Benue Trough.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Explain the origin of the Cretaceous sedimentary basins of Cameroon.",
           "In the Early Cretaceous Gondwana broke apart. Stretching of the lithosphere produced normal faults and rift basins: the South Atlantic and Gulf of Guinea margins on the coast, and the West and Central African Rift System inland (Benue Trough, with branches such as the Garoua and Mamfe basins). Old Pan-African shear zones guided the rifts. On the coast, rifting succeeded, an ocean opened and the margin became a passive margin with thermal subsidence; inland, the rifts failed."),
        PQ("Compare the coastal and inland Cretaceous basins of Cameroon.",
           "Coastal (Rio del Rey, Douala/Kribi-Campo): passive margins on the Gulf of Guinea, syn-rift followed by thick post-rift marine and deltaic sediments, Cretaceous to Cenozoic, oil and gas producing. Inland (Mamfe, Garoua): failed rifts linked to the Benue Trough, mostly continental syn-rift sandstones and conglomerates, thinner, little or no production. Both rest on Precambrian basement."),
        PQ("A sequence 1 800 m thick accumulated in 45 million years (invented figures). Calculate the average rate in m per million years and suggest why the true rate of deposition was higher.",
           "1 800 ÷ 45 = 40 m per million years (0.04 mm per year). The true rate was higher because the sediment has been compacted (it was thicker when deposited), and because there were probably pauses or erosion surfaces in the sequence."),
        SUM("The break-up of Gondwana in the Cretaceous opened the South Atlantic and the West and Central African rifts.",
            "Rift basins hold wedge-shaped syn-rift fill; passive margins add a thick post-rift sequence.",
            "Coastal basins: Rio del Rey and Douala/Kribi-Campo, the oil and gas basins.",
            "Inland basins: Mamfe and Garoua, failed rifts with continental fill linked to the Benue Trough; the Lake Chad basin in the Far North.",
            "Sedimentation rate = thickness ÷ time, an underestimate because of compaction and gaps."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 11. The Cameroon Volcanic Line
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_STR,
    topic="The Cameroon Volcanic Line",
    title="The Cameroon Volcanic Line: structure, rocks, hazards and origin",
    slug="gyu2-cameroon-volcanic-line",
    minutes=25,
    summary="The Cameroon Volcanic Line is a chain of Cenozoic volcanoes and plutons about 1 600 km long, from the Gulf of Guinea islands to the Lake Chad area, crossing the ocean-continent boundary. Its alkaline rocks, lack of age progression and hazards make it a classic intraplate problem.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the extent, trend and main volcanoes of the Cameroon Volcanic Line",
        "Describe its alkaline rocks and the ocean-continent setting",
        "Evaluate the hotspot, hot-line and lithospheric models for its origin",
        "Explain the volcanic and gas hazards of the line and how they are managed",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Intraplate volcanism", "Alkaline basalt", "Trachyte", "Phonolite", "Hotspot", "Age progression", "Maar", "Limnic eruption"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("From Buea you can see the slopes of **Mount Cameroon**, the highest mountain in West and Central Africa (just over 4 000 m) and one of the most active volcanoes of the continent; it erupted in 1999 and 2000. It is one volcano in a long chain, the **Cameroon Volcanic Line (CVL)**, which crosses the country from the coast to the north. This lesson describes the line and asks a question geologists still debate: why is it there?"),
        D("The **Cameroon Volcanic Line** is a roughly N30°E trending chain of Cenozoic volcanoes and anorogenic plutonic complexes about 1 600 km long. Its oceanic part includes the islands of the Gulf of Guinea (Annobón, São Tomé, Príncipe and Bioko); its continental part includes Mount Cameroon, Mount Manengouba, the Bamboutos Mountains, Mount Oku and the Adamawa plateau volcanism, continuing towards the Biu plateau and the Lake Chad area.", title="The Cameroon Volcanic Line"),
        IMG("algyu2-cvl.svg", alt="Schematic Cameroon Volcanic Line as a band trending north-east: oceanic islands (Annobón, São Tomé, Príncipe, Bioko) in the south-west, the coastline crossing the line near Mount Cameroon, and continental volcanoes (Manengouba, Bamboutos, Oku, Adamawa) to the north-east",
            caption="Figure 1. Schematic layout of the Cameroon Volcanic Line (not a map: positions and spacing are simplified)."),
        H("A line across two kinds of lithosphere"),
        T("The CVL is unusual because it crosses the **continent-ocean boundary**: the same kind of volcanism occurs on oceanic lithosphere in the Gulf of Guinea and on thick continental lithosphere in Cameroon. It is **intraplate**: there is no subduction zone or mid-ocean ridge here. The volcanism has been active, in various places, from the Eocene to the present (check your textbook for ages)."),
        KEY("Rocks of the CVL", "An alkaline suite.", cards=[
            ["Basic lavas", "**Alkali basalts** and **basanites**, poor in silica and rich in sodium and potassium, with olivine and titanium-rich augite. Many contain **xenoliths** of mantle peridotite, showing that the magma rose quickly from the mantle."],
            ["Evolved lavas", "**Trachytes** and **phonolites**, formed by fractional crystallization of basaltic magma in crustal chambers; they build domes and plugs (for example the plugs often cited in the Mandara and Kapsiki area: check your textbook)."],
            ["Plutonic complexes", "Anorogenic ring complexes of syenite and granite along the line, older than most of the lavas."],
            ["Volcanic forms", "Large shield-like stratovolcanoes, cinder cones, lava flows and **maars** (explosion craters, often holding crater lakes)."],
        ]),
        H("Why is the line there? Competing models"),
        KEY("Models for the origin", "Each explains some evidence; none explains all.", cards=[
            ["Hotspot (mantle plume)", "A plume under a moving plate makes a chain of volcanoes. Problem: a hotspot track should show an **age progression**, with volcanoes older in one direction. The CVL has no simple age progression, and the African plate has moved little relative to the mantle."],
            ["Hot line", "A linear zone of hot upwelling mantle under the whole line. Explains the line's shape and long-lived activity, but the cause of such a line is unclear."],
            ["Lithospheric weakness", "Decompression melting where the lithosphere is weakened along old Pan-African shear zones (such as the Central Cameroon Shear Zone) or along the Cretaceous rift structures, which are reactivated by plate stresses."],
            ["Edge-driven convection", "Small-scale mantle convection at the edge of the thick, cold Congo Craton draws up hot mantle that melts as it rises."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: testing the hotspot model", intro="If the African plate moved over a fixed plume at 2 cm per year for 30 million years, how long would the resulting chain of volcanoes be, and what age pattern would it show? Compare with the CVL.", steps=[
            S("Distance", math=r"0.02 \text{ m/yr} \times 30\,000\,000 \text{ yr} = 600\,000 \text{ m} = 600 \text{ km}"),
            S("Expected pattern", text="A 600 km chain with ages increasing steadily away from the active end."),
            S("Comparison", text="The CVL is about 1 600 km long, with young volcanoes at both ends and in the middle and no steady age progression. The simple hotspot model does not fit; lithospheric control or a hot-line model fits better."),
        ]),
        H("Hazards"),
        T("Mount Cameroon's eruptions produce mainly basaltic **lava flows** from fissures on its flanks, which can cut roads and destroy farms and buildings, as in 1999, when lava approached the coast. Earthquakes, ash and gas accompany eruptions, and steep slopes are prone to landslides in the rainy season."),
        T("The deadliest hazard was not an eruption. Carbon dioxide from the mantle seeps into the bottom water of some deep crater lakes. In 1984 a gas release from **Lake Monoun** killed 37 people, and in 1986 a sudden release from **Lake Nyos** killed about 1 700 people and thousands of cattle. This kind of event is called a **limnic eruption**. Since 2001, degassing pipes have been installed to bring the gas-rich bottom water to the surface slowly and reduce the risk."),
        EX("Worked example 2: explaining a limnic eruption", intro="Explain why the Lake Nyos disaster happened.", steps=[
            S("Gas supply", text="Magmatic CO2 entered the deep water of the maar lake through springs."),
            S("Storage", text="The lake was stratified and deep, so the bottom water held a large amount of dissolved CO2 under pressure."),
            S("Trigger", text="A disturbance (perhaps a landslide, wind or cooling of surface water; the cause is debated) lifted bottom water; with less pressure the gas came out of solution rapidly."),
            S("Effect", text="CO2 is denser than air, so the cloud flowed down valleys into villages and suffocated people and animals."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling the CVL a plate boundary", "The CVL is intraplate: it lies within the African plate, with no subduction or spreading. Its volcanoes are alkaline, unlike the calc-alkaline volcanoes of subduction zones."),
        MISTAKE("Stating one model as fact", "The origin of the CVL is debated. Present the evidence for and against each model and give a reasoned conclusion."),
        QUIZ("Which observation is the strongest argument against a simple hotspot origin for the CVL?", ["Its lavas are alkali basalts", "It has no steady age progression along its length", "It contains mantle xenoliths", "It includes maar lakes"], 1,
             "A plate moving over a fixed plume should give an age progression.", hint="What does a hotspot track look like?",
             feedback=["Alkali basalts are also found at many hotspots.", "", "Xenoliths show rapid ascent, not the cause of melting.", "Maars are a style of eruption, not evidence of origin."]),
        QUIZ("What killed most victims at Lake Nyos in 1986?", ["Hot lava flows", "Suffocation by carbon dioxide", "A tsunami from the lake", "Volcanic ash falls"], 1,
             "Dense CO2 flowed downhill and displaced the air.", hint="What gas was dissolved in the lake?",
             feedback=["There was no lava.", "", "A wave occurred in the lake but the deaths were by asphyxiation.", "There was no ash fall."]),
        TIP("The CVL is a favourite for Paper 2 essays (origin, rocks, hazards) and for Paper 1. Always name examples, use the term intraplate, and evaluate the models rather than listing them.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe the Cameroon Volcanic Line.",
           "A chain of Cenozoic volcanoes and plutons about 1 600 km long trending about N30°E from Annobón through São Tomé, Príncipe and Bioko to Mount Cameroon, Manengouba, Bamboutos, Oku and the Adamawa, towards Lake Chad. It crosses the ocean-continent boundary and is intraplate. Rocks are alkaline: alkali basalts and basanites with mantle xenoliths, and trachytes and phonolites; plus anorogenic ring complexes. Forms include stratovolcanoes, cinder cones, lava flows and maars. Mount Cameroon is active."),
        PQ("Evaluate the main hypotheses for the origin of the Cameroon Volcanic Line.",
           "Hotspot: explains alkaline basalts and long activity, but there is no simple age progression and the plate has moved little, so it fails in its simple form. Hot line: explains the linear shape and activity along the whole line, but does not explain why the upwelling is linear. Lithospheric control: melting by decompression along reactivated Pan-African shear zones or Cretaceous rift structures explains the trend parallel to old structures and the lack of age progression. Edge-driven convection at the Congo Craton margin explains why the line follows the craton edge. Conclusion: most authors favour a combination of mantle upwelling and lithospheric control; the question remains open."),
        PQ("Suggest measures to reduce the risks from Mount Cameroon and from gas-rich crater lakes.",
           "Mount Cameroon: monitoring of earthquakes, ground deformation and gas; hazard maps of past lava flows; land-use planning to keep settlements and roads out of likely flow paths; evacuation plans and education. Lakes: controlled degassing with pipes (as at Nyos and Monoun), monitoring of CO2 levels, warning systems, keeping people out of low ground near the lakes, and reinforcing natural dams that could fail."),
        SUM("The CVL is a 1 600 km, N30°E chain of Cenozoic intraplate volcanoes and plutons from the Gulf of Guinea to the Lake Chad area.",
            "It crosses the ocean-continent boundary and erupts alkaline magmas: alkali basalt, basanite, trachyte, phonolite.",
            "No simple age progression argues against a simple hotspot; lithospheric control and mantle upwelling are favoured.",
            "Mount Cameroon is active (1999, 2000); Lakes Monoun (1984) and Nyos (1986) released deadly CO2.",
            "Monitoring, hazard maps, planning and degassing reduce the risks."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 12. Ore deposits
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ECO,
    topic="Ore deposits",
    title="Ore deposits: magmatic, hydrothermal, sedimentary, residual and supergene",
    slug="gyu2-ore-deposits",
    minutes=26,
    summary="An ore deposit concentrates a metal far above its crustal abundance. Magmatic segregation, hydrothermal fluids, sedimentary and placer processes, residual weathering and supergene enrichment each concentrate metals; grade, tonnage and price decide whether it can be mined.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate concentration factors and metal content from grade and tonnage",
        "Explain magmatic and hydrothermal ore-forming processes and their geological settings",
        "Describe placer, residual (laterite and bauxite) and supergene enrichment deposits",
        "Relate Cameroon's main mineral prospects to these processes, with hedged localities",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Concentration factor", "Grade and cut-off grade", "Magmatic segregation", "Hydrothermal", "Wall-rock alteration", "Placer", "Laterite", "Supergene enrichment", "Gossan"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Copper makes up only about 0.005% of the Earth's crust. A mine needs rock with roughly a hundred times more. Something must have concentrated the metal. At O Level you learnt the names of ore-forming processes; at A Level you must explain **how** each works, where it happens and how to judge whether a deposit is worth mining."),
        D("An **ore** is rock from which a metal or mineral can be extracted at a profit. The **grade** is the concentration of metal in the ore (per cent, or grams per tonne). The **cut-off grade** is the lowest grade that can be mined at a profit. The **concentration factor** is the ratio of ore grade to the average crustal abundance of the metal.", title="Ore, grade and concentration factor"),
        F(r"\text{concentration factor} = \frac{\text{grade of ore}}{\text{average crustal abundance}}", title="Concentration factor"),
        EX("Worked example 1: concentration factors", intro="Average crustal abundances (teaching values): copper 0.005%, aluminium 8%, gold 0.004 g per tonne. Typical ores: copper 0.5%, bauxite 30% Al, gold 4 g per tonne. Calculate each concentration factor.", steps=[
            S("Copper", math=r"0.5 \div 0.005 = 100"),
            S("Aluminium", math=r"30 \div 8 = 3.75"),
            S("Gold", math=r"4 \div 0.004 = 1\,000"),
            S("Interpret", text="Rare metals such as gold need huge concentration; aluminium, already common, needs little."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: grade and tonnage", intro="A deposit contains 2 million tonnes of ore at 1.5% copper. The plant recovers 85% of the metal. How much copper is produced?", steps=[
            S("Metal in the ore", math=r"2\,000\,000 \times 0.015 = 30\,000 \text{ t}"),
            S("Recovered", math=r"30\,000 \times 0.85 = 25\,500 \text{ t}"),
        ]),
        H("Magmatic deposits"),
        KEY("Concentration inside a magma", "Ore forms as the magma crystallizes.", cards=[
            ["Crystal settling (segregation)", "Dense early minerals such as **chromite** and magnetite sink and form layers at the base of basic and ultrabasic intrusions."],
            ["Immiscible sulphide liquid", "In some basic magmas a sulphide liquid separates like oil from water, collects nickel, copper and platinum and sinks to form massive sulphide ores."],
            ["Pegmatites", "The last, water-rich residual melt of granites crystallizes very coarse rocks rich in rare elements (lithium, tin, tantalum, beryllium) and gem minerals."],
        ]),
        H("Hydrothermal deposits"),
        D("**Hydrothermal** ores are deposited from hot, watery fluids moving through cracks and pores. The fluids may come from a cooling magma, from heated groundwater or seawater, or from metamorphic dewatering. They carry metals as chloride or sulphide complexes and deposit them when temperature, pressure or chemistry change.", title="Hydrothermal deposit"),
        KEY("Main hydrothermal settings", "Learn one example of each type.", cards=[
            ["Veins", "Fluids fill fractures with quartz and ore minerals (gold, galena, sphalerite, chalcopyrite). Classified by temperature of formation into high-, medium- and low-temperature veins."],
            ["Porphyry deposits", "Large, low-grade copper (molybdenum, gold) deposits disseminated in and around porphyritic granitic intrusions above subduction zones."],
            ["Greisen and skarn", "Fluorine- and boron-rich fluids from granites alter the granite itself (greisen, with tin and tungsten minerals) or react with limestone (skarn)."],
            ["Seafloor (VMS)", "Hot fluids venting at mid-ocean ridges ('black smokers') deposit massive copper-zinc sulphides on the sea floor."],
        ]),
        T("The rock around a hydrothermal deposit is altered by the fluid (**wall-rock alteration**): feldspars turn to sericite or clay, and silica or chlorite is added. Mapping alteration zones helps to find hidden ore."),
        H("Sedimentary, placer and residual deposits"),
        T("**Sedimentary** ores are deposited from water as chemical sediments, for example banded iron formations and evaporite salts. **Placers** form where dense, resistant minerals (gold, cassiterite, rutile, diamond) are concentrated by flowing water: on the inside of meander bends, in potholes, at the base of gravels and on beaches. **Residual** deposits form by deep tropical weathering, which leaches soluble elements and leaves insoluble ones: **bauxite** (aluminium) and **lateritic** iron, nickel, cobalt and manganese."),
        H("Supergene enrichment"),
        IMG("algyu2-supergene.svg", alt="Vertical profile through a weathered sulphide ore body: at the top a gossan of iron oxides, below it a leached zone, then an oxidized zone, then the water table, below that a zone of supergene enrichment rich in secondary copper sulphides, and at the bottom the unweathered primary ore",
            caption="Figure 1. Supergene enrichment of a copper sulphide deposit (schematic)."),
        T("Above the water table, rainwater with oxygen oxidizes primary sulphides such as pyrite and chalcopyrite. This makes sulphuric acid, which dissolves copper and carries it down. The iron stays behind as a red-brown cap of iron oxides, the **gossan**, a useful guide for prospectors. At and below the water table, where there is little oxygen, the copper is precipitated as secondary sulphides (chalcocite, covellite) that replace the primary ones. The result is a **zone of supergene enrichment** with a grade often several times that of the primary ore."),
        H("Cameroon's mineral prospects"),
        T("Localities below are those commonly cited; the status of mines and projects changes, so check your textbook and current information. Bauxite on the Adamawa plateau (Minim-Martap and Ngaoundal) and in the West (Fongo-Tongo) is a residual deposit. Cobalt-nickel-manganese laterites near Lomié in the East are residual. Iron ore at Mbalam and other southern prospects is in banded iron formations of the craton. Gold in the East (Bétaré-Oya, Batouri) is hydrothermal (quartz veins) and placer. Rutile near Akonolinga is in residual and placer deposits."),
        MISTAKE("Defining ore without profit", "An ore must be economic. A rock with metal that cannot be extracted at a profit is a mineral deposit, not an ore."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the gossan and the enriched zone", "The gossan is the leached, iron-oxide cap at the surface. The enriched zone lies at and below the water table, where copper is redeposited."),
        QUIZ("In supergene enrichment, where is copper redeposited as secondary sulphides?", ["In the gossan at the surface", "In the leached zone", "At and just below the water table", "Deep in the unweathered granite"], 2,
             "Below the water table oxygen is scarce, so copper sulphides are stable.", hint="Where does oxidizing water stop?",
             feedback=["The gossan is iron oxide left after copper is removed.", "Copper is removed from the leached zone.", "", "The primary zone is the unaltered ore below the enrichment."]),
        QUIZ("Chromite layers at the base of an ultrabasic intrusion formed by:", ["Supergene enrichment", "Crystal settling (magmatic segregation)", "Placer concentration", "Evaporation"], 1,
             "Dense early-formed chromite sank through the magma.", hint="Which process happens inside the magma?",
             feedback=["Supergene enrichment is a weathering process.", "", "Placers form in rivers and beaches.", "Evaporation forms salts."]),
        TIP("Ore genesis is common in Paper 2 essays and Paper 1. The Board's examiners have noted weak answers on processes linked to contact metamorphism, such as greisening, tourmalinization and tin-tungsten mineralization: learn them with hydrothermal deposits.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Explain, with a labelled diagram, the formation of a zone of supergene enrichment.",
           "Diagram: gossan, leached zone, oxidized zone, water table, enriched zone, primary zone. Above the water table oxygenated rainwater oxidizes pyrite and chalcopyrite, forming sulphuric acid that dissolves copper; iron remains as oxides (gossan). The copper-bearing solution percolates down. At the water table, under reducing conditions, copper precipitates as chalcocite and covellite, replacing primary sulphides, so the grade rises. Below lies unaltered primary ore."),
        PQ("Describe two magmatic and two hydrothermal processes of ore formation, naming a metal for each.",
           "Magmatic: crystal settling of dense chromite (chromium) in ultrabasic intrusions; separation of an immiscible sulphide liquid concentrating nickel and copper. Hydrothermal: vein filling by quartz with gold or galena (lead); porphyry deposits of disseminated copper around granitic intrusions; greisen with cassiterite (tin) and wolframite (tungsten); black smoker massive sulphides (copper, zinc)."),
        PQ("A gold placer contains 0.8 g of gold per tonne of gravel; crustal abundance is 0.004 g per tonne (teaching values). Calculate the concentration factor and explain why gold forms placers.",
           "0.8 ÷ 0.004 = 200. Gold is very dense (about 19 g per cm³), chemically inert and malleable, so it survives weathering and transport, and it settles where the current slows: inside meander bends, potholes, the base of gravels."),
        SUM("Concentration factor = ore grade ÷ crustal abundance; grade, tonnage, recovery and price decide whether a deposit is an ore.",
            "Magmatic ores form by crystal settling, immiscible sulphide liquids and pegmatites.",
            "Hydrothermal ores form in veins, porphyries, greisens, skarns and seafloor vents, with wall-rock alteration.",
            "Placers concentrate dense resistant minerals; residual weathering makes bauxite and laterites.",
            "Supergene enrichment leaches copper above the water table and redeposits it at and below it, under a gossan."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 13. Petroleum geology
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ECO,
    topic="Petroleum geology",
    title="Petroleum systems: source, maturation, migration, reservoir, seal and trap",
    slug="gyu2-petroleum-geology",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Oil and gas form when organic-rich source rocks are buried and heated into the oil and gas windows. They migrate into porous, permeable reservoirs and are held in structural or stratigraphic traps below a seal. Porosity, geothermal gradient and volume calculations judge a prospect.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe source rocks, kerogen types and the maturation of organic matter",
        "Calculate the depth of the oil window from the geothermal gradient",
        "Explain primary and secondary migration and the properties of reservoir and cap rocks",
        "Classify structural and stratigraphic traps and estimate oil in place",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Source rock", "Kerogen", "Oil window", "Migration", "Porosity", "Permeability", "Cap rock", "Structural trap", "Stratigraphic trap"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Cameroon produces oil and gas from its coastal basins, and much of the country's export income has come from them. Finding oil is expensive: one offshore well can cost as much as a large hospital. Geologists therefore check that every part of a **petroleum system** is present before drilling. This lesson takes each part in turn."),
        D("A **petroleum system** is the set of geological elements and processes needed for oil and gas to accumulate: a mature **source rock**, **migration** pathways, a **reservoir rock**, a **seal** (cap rock) and a **trap**, formed in the right order (the trap must exist before the oil migrates).", title="Petroleum system"),
        H("Source rocks and maturation"),
        T("A **source rock** is a fine-grained sediment, usually a dark shale or limestone, rich in organic matter (often more than about 1% total organic carbon). The organic matter accumulated where productivity was high and the bottom water was **anoxic**, so it was not destroyed. With burial it turns into **kerogen**, an insoluble mixture of large organic molecules."),
        KEY("Kerogen types", "The type of organic matter decides the product.", cards=[
            ["Type I", "From algae in lakes; very rich in hydrogen; mainly oil."],
            ["Type II", "From marine plankton and bacteria; the main source of most of the world's oil."],
            ["Type III", "From woody land plants; poor in hydrogen; mainly gas (and coal)."],
        ]),
        D("The **oil window** is the range of temperature (roughly 60 to 120 °C, values differ between textbooks) at which kerogen cracks to liquid oil. Above it, oil and kerogen crack to **gas** (the gas window, up to roughly 200 °C). Below it, the rock is immature, though bacteria can make biogenic gas.", title="Oil and gas windows"),
        F(r"\text{depth} = \frac{T - T_{\text{surface}}}{\text{geothermal gradient}}", title="Depth to a temperature"),
        EX("Worked example 1: depth of the oil window", intro="The mean surface temperature is 25 °C and the geothermal gradient is 30 °C per km. Using an oil window of 60 to 120 °C, find its top and base.", steps=[
            S("Top", math=r"\frac{60 - 25}{30} = 1.17 \text{ km}"),
            S("Base", math=r"\frac{120 - 25}{30} = 3.17 \text{ km}"),
            S("Interpret", text="A source rock must be buried between about 1.2 and 3.2 km to generate oil here. In a basin with a higher gradient the window is shallower."),
        ]),
        H("Migration"),
        T("**Primary migration** is the expulsion of oil and gas out of the fine-grained source rock, driven by compaction and the pressure of the newly formed fluid. **Secondary migration** is movement through permeable carrier beds and faults towards a trap. Oil and gas are less dense than water, so they rise by **buoyancy** until stopped by an impermeable barrier."),
        H("Reservoirs and seals"),
        D("**Porosity** is the percentage of a rock's volume that is pore space. **Permeability** is the ability of the rock to let fluids flow through it; it depends on the connection between pores. A good reservoir needs both.", title="Porosity and permeability", formula=r"\phi = \frac{V_{\text{pores}}}{V_{\text{total}}} \times 100\%"),
        T("Typical reservoirs are well-sorted sandstones (deltaic, beach and turbidite sands, as in the Gulf of Guinea basins) and porous or fractured limestones and reefs. Porosity is reduced by compaction and cementation, and increased by dissolution and fracturing. The **seal** or cap rock is an impermeable layer, usually shale or evaporite (salt is the best seal)."),
        H("Traps"),
        IMG("algyu2-traps.svg", alt="Four petroleum traps: an anticline with oil at its crest under shale; a fault trap with sandstone sealed against shale; a salt dome with oil in upturned sandstone beside it; and a stratigraphic pinch-out where a sandstone wedge thins out within shale",
            caption="Figure 1. Structural traps (anticline, fault, salt dome) and a stratigraphic trap (pinch-out). Schematic."),
        KEY("Kinds of trap", "Structural traps are made by deformation; stratigraphic traps by changes in the rocks themselves.", cards=[
            ["Anticline", "Oil and gas collect at the crest of a fold, below the seal. Gas sits above oil, oil above water. The commonest trap."],
            ["Fault trap", "A reservoir bed is displaced against an impermeable bed across a fault."],
            ["Salt dome", "Rising salt bends up the surrounding beds and seals them against its flanks; the cap above also forms domes."],
            ["Stratigraphic traps", "A reservoir sand thins out (pinch-out) or changes into shale; a reservoir is cut by an **unconformity** and sealed by the beds above; a buried **reef** sealed by shale."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: oil in place", intro="An anticlinal trap covers 4 km², with 20 m of net reservoir, porosity 20% and oil saturation 70% (invented teaching figures). Estimate the volume of oil in place.", steps=[
            S("Rock volume", math=r"4 \times 10^{6} \text{ m}^2 \times 20 \text{ m} = 8 \times 10^{7} \text{ m}^3"),
            S("Pore volume", math=r"8 \times 10^{7} \times 0.20 = 1.6 \times 10^{7} \text{ m}^3"),
            S("Oil volume", math=r"1.6 \times 10^{7} \times 0.70 = 1.12 \times 10^{7} \text{ m}^3"),
            S("Comment", text="Only part of this (often 20 to 50%) can be recovered; the rest stays in the pores."),
        ]),
        T("In Cameroon the main producing areas are the Rio del Rey basin and the Douala/Kribi-Campo basin, where Cretaceous and Tertiary marine shales are the source and deltaic and turbidite sands the reservoirs (check your textbook). Exploration and production are overseen by the national hydrocarbons company, the SNH."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing porosity with permeability", "Clay can have high porosity but very low permeability, because its pores are tiny and poorly connected. A reservoir needs connected pores."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting timing", "A trap formed after the oil migrated past is empty. Always state that the trap must form before or during migration."),
        QUIZ("In an anticlinal trap, which order of fluids is found from the crest downwards?", ["Water, oil, gas", "Gas, oil, water", "Oil, gas, water", "Oil, water, gas"], 1,
             "The fluids separate by density: gas (lightest) at the top, then oil, then water.", hint="Order by density.",
             feedback=["That is upside down.", "", "Gas is lighter than oil.", "Water is denser than oil and gas."]),
        QUIZ("A source rock is at 100 °C. The surface is at 25 °C and the gradient 25 °C per km. How deep is it?", ["2 km", "3 km", "4 km", "5 km"], 1,
             "(100 − 25) ÷ 25 = 3 km.", hint="Subtract the surface temperature first.",
             feedback=["That would be at 75 °C.", "", "100 ÷ 25 = 4 forgets the surface temperature.", "That would be at 150 °C."]),
        TIP("Petroleum geology appears in Paper 1 (terms, traps, calculations) and Paper 2 (essays on the petroleum system, with diagrams of traps). Draw traps with the seal, reservoir, gas, oil and water labelled.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe the conditions needed for the formation and accumulation of petroleum.",
           "Organic-rich fine sediment deposited under anoxic conditions (source rock); burial to the oil window (about 60 to 120 °C) so kerogen matures; primary migration out of the source and secondary migration through carrier beds and faults; a porous and permeable reservoir (sandstone, limestone); an impermeable seal (shale, salt); a trap (structural or stratigraphic) formed before migration; and no later heating, uplift or faulting that destroys the accumulation."),
        PQ("With labelled diagrams, describe two structural traps and two stratigraphic traps.",
           "Structural: anticline (oil at crest under shale seal, gas-oil and oil-water contacts); fault trap (reservoir sealed against shale across fault); salt dome (beds upturned and sealed against salt). Stratigraphic: pinch-out (sand wedge thins into shale); unconformity trap (tilted reservoir truncated by unconformity and sealed by overlying shale); reef trap (buried reef limestone sealed by shale)."),
        PQ("A sandstone core of volume 250 cm³ contains 55 cm³ of connected pore space. Calculate its porosity and state whether it is a good reservoir.",
           "Porosity = 55 ÷ 250 × 100 = 22%. That is good reservoir porosity, provided the pores are connected so that permeability is also high (well-sorted, weakly cemented sand)."),
        SUM("A petroleum system needs a mature source rock, migration, a reservoir, a seal and a trap, in the right order.",
            "Kerogen type I and II give mainly oil, type III mainly gas.",
            "Oil window roughly 60 to 120 °C; depth = (T − surface T) ÷ geothermal gradient.",
            "Reservoirs need porosity and permeability; shale and salt are seals.",
            "Traps: anticline, fault and salt dome (structural); pinch-out, unconformity and reef (stratigraphic)."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 14. Hydrogeology
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ECO,
    topic="Hydrogeology",
    title="Hydrogeology: aquifers, Darcy's law and groundwater flow",
    slug="gyu2-hydrogeology-darcy",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Groundwater flows through aquifers as Darcy's law, Q = KA Δh/L, describes. Conductivity, gradient, specific yield and transmissivity describe aquifers; pumping makes cones of depression; and confined, unconfined and basement aquifers face overpumping, pollution and saline intrusion.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish porosity, specific yield, specific retention and hydraulic conductivity",
        "Apply Darcy's law to calculate discharge, hydraulic gradient and flow velocity",
        "Describe unconfined, confined and perched aquifers, aquitards and transmissivity",
        "Explain drawdown, cones of depression, pollution and saline intrusion, with Cameroonian examples",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Aquifer", "Aquitard", "Hydraulic conductivity", "Hydraulic gradient", "Darcy's law", "Specific yield", "Transmissivity", "Potentiometric surface", "Cone of depression"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In many towns and villages of Cameroon, drinking water comes from wells and boreholes. Some boreholes yield plenty of water; others, a few hundred metres away, are dry. Hydrogeology explains why. At O Level you met aquifers and artesian wells; at A Level you must calculate flow with Darcy's law and explain how aquifers respond to pumping."),
        D("An **aquifer** is a rock or sediment that stores and transmits enough water to supply wells and springs. An **aquitard** transmits water only slowly; an **aquiclude** is effectively impermeable. **Porosity** is the fraction of pore space; **specific yield** is the fraction of the rock volume that drains out by gravity; **specific retention** is the fraction held back by surface tension. Porosity = specific yield + specific retention.", title="Aquifers and storage"),
        T("Clay has a high porosity (often over 40%) but a tiny specific yield, because water clings to its small grains. Well-sorted gravel and sand have both good porosity and high specific yield, so they are the best aquifers. Crystalline rocks like granite have very little primary porosity, but their **weathered zone** and **fractures** can store and transmit water."),
        H("Darcy's law"),
        D("In 1856 the French engineer Henry Darcy found that the rate of flow of water through a sand column is proportional to the cross-sectional area and to the difference in hydraulic head, and inversely proportional to the length of the flow path.", title="Darcy's law", formula=r"Q = K A \frac{\Delta h}{L}"),
        T(r"Here $Q$ is the discharge (m³ per day), $K$ the **hydraulic conductivity** (m per day), $A$ the cross-sectional area (m²), $\Delta h$ the difference in hydraulic head (m) and $L$ the length of the flow path (m). The ratio $\Delta h / L$ is the **hydraulic gradient**, $i$. The **hydraulic head** at a point is the height to which water rises in a well open at that point."),
        IMG("algyu2-darcy.svg", alt="Cross-section: an unconfined sand aquifer with a sloping water table above a confined sandstone aquifer between clay layers; its potentiometric surface (dashed) lies above the valley floor, where an artesian well flows; two wells a distance L apart differ in level by delta h",
            caption="Figure 1. Unconfined and confined aquifers. The head difference Δh between two wells a distance L apart drives flow (schematic)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: discharge through an aquifer", intro="A sand aquifer has K = 10 m per day. Two wells 100 m apart have water levels differing by 2 m. Calculate the discharge through a cross-section of 50 m².", steps=[
            S("Hydraulic gradient", math=r"i = \frac{2}{100} = 0.02"),
            S("Darcy's law", math=r"Q = 10 \times 50 \times 0.02 = 10 \text{ m}^3 \text{ per day}"),
        ], check=r"\frac{Q}{KA} = \frac{10}{500} = 0.02 = i"),
        T("The **Darcy velocity** $q = Q/A = Ki$ is not the real speed of the water, because water flows only through the pores. The average linear (seepage) velocity is $v = Ki/n_e$, where $n_e$ is the effective porosity. Pollutants travel at roughly this speed."),
        EX("Worked example 2: how fast does a pollutant move?", intro="Using the aquifer above (K = 10 m per day, i = 0.02) and an effective porosity of 0.25, how long would a pollutant take to travel 100 m from a pit latrine to a well?", steps=[
            S("Seepage velocity", math=r"v = \frac{10 \times 0.02}{0.25} = 0.8 \text{ m per day}"),
            S("Time", math=r"100 \div 0.8 = 125 \text{ days}"),
            S("Comment", text="About four months; in a fractured rock with low effective porosity it could be much faster."),
        ]),
        H("Aquifer types and behaviour"),
        KEY("Kinds of aquifer", "Know how each is recharged.", cards=[
            ["Unconfined", "Open to the surface; the top of the saturated zone is the **water table**; recharged by rain over its whole area."],
            ["Confined", "Between aquitards; water is under pressure, so it rises in a well to the **potentiometric surface**. If that surface is above the ground, the well flows (artesian). Recharged where the aquifer crops out."],
            ["Perched", "A lens of saturated rock above a small clay layer, above the main water table; small and unreliable."],
            ["Fractured basement", "Weathered regolith over fractured granite or gneiss. Yields depend on finding fractures; common in most of Cameroon outside the sedimentary basins."],
        ]),
        D("**Transmissivity** $T = Kb$ is the rate at which water passes through the whole thickness $b$ of an aquifer per unit width under unit gradient (m² per day). When a well is pumped, the water level around it falls in a **cone of depression**; the fall at the well is the **drawdown**.", title="Transmissivity and drawdown"),
        T("Problems: **overpumping** lowers the water table and dries up shallow wells; **pollution** from latrines, waste dumps, fuel and fertilizers enters shallow aquifers; and in coastal aquifers, such as those under parts of Douala, heavy pumping can draw in seawater (**saline intrusion**). Protection zones around wells, safe distances from latrines, and controlled pumping reduce these risks."),
        MISTAKE("Using porosity as the water a rock can give", "The water a well can extract depends on specific yield (and permeability), not total porosity. Clay is porous but yields almost nothing."),
        MISTAKE("Dropping units in Darcy's law", "Keep K, A, Δh and L in consistent units (metres and days). Write the units in each line of the calculation."),
        QUIZ("A sandstone aquifer has K = 5 m per day and is 20 m thick. What is its transmissivity?", ["0.25 m² per day", "4 m² per day", "25 m² per day", "100 m² per day"], 3,
             "T = Kb = 5 × 20 = 100 m² per day.", hint="Multiply conductivity by thickness.",
             feedback=["That is 5 ÷ 20.", "That is 20 ÷ 5.", "That is 5 + 20.", ""]),
        QUIZ("Water in a well in a confined aquifer rises above the top of the aquifer because:", ["The aquifer is open to the air", "The water is under pressure from the head at the recharge area", "Capillary action lifts it", "The pump pushes it up"], 1,
             "Water in the confined aquifer is under the pressure of the higher recharge area, so it rises to the potentiometric surface.", hint="Where is the recharge area?",
             feedback=["A confined aquifer is sealed above by an aquitard.", "", "Capillary rise is only a few centimetres to metres in fine sediments.", "An artesian well can flow without a pump."]),
        TIP("Darcy's law calculations are natural Paper 1 and Paper 2 questions. Always state the formula, the gradient and the units. In essays, link the aquifer type to the local geology (sedimentary basin or weathered basement).", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("In a laboratory, water flows through a sand column 0.5 m long with a cross-section of 0.02 m². The head difference is 0.3 m and the discharge is 0.6 m³ per day. Calculate the hydraulic conductivity.",
           "K = QL ÷ (AΔh) = (0.6 × 0.5) ÷ (0.02 × 0.3) = 0.3 ÷ 0.006 = 50 m per day, typical of a clean sand."),
        PQ("Distinguish between a confined and an unconfined aquifer and explain how an artesian well works.",
           "Unconfined: open to the surface, upper limit is the water table, recharged over its whole area, water at atmospheric pressure. Confined: lies between aquitards, recharged where it crops out, water under pressure, level in wells rises to the potentiometric surface. Artesian well: the aquifer dips from a high recharge area; the head at the well is set by the recharge area, so where the potentiometric surface lies above the ground the well flows without pumping."),
        PQ("Explain why boreholes in crystalline basement rocks often have unreliable yields, and how a hydrogeologist can improve the success rate.",
           "Fresh granite and gneiss have very low primary porosity and permeability; water is stored in the weathered regolith and moves in fractures, which are irregular. A borehole that misses fractures is dry. Success improves by siting boreholes on fracture zones identified from air photos, satellite images and lineaments, by geophysical surveys (resistivity, electromagnetic) to find thick weathered zones and fractures, and by drilling deep enough to cross the saturated regolith."),
        SUM("Porosity = specific yield + specific retention; good aquifers combine high specific yield with high permeability.",
            r"Darcy's law: $Q = KA\,\Delta h/L$; the hydraulic gradient is $\Delta h/L$.",
            "Seepage velocity = Ki ÷ effective porosity; it sets the speed of pollutants.",
            "Unconfined, confined, perched and fractured basement aquifers behave differently; T = Kb.",
            "Pumping causes drawdown and cones of depression; overpumping, pollution and saline intrusion must be managed."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 15. Engineering geology
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ECO,
    topic="Engineering geology",
    title="Engineering geology: rock and soil properties for dams, tunnels, foundations and quarries",
    slug="gyu2-engineering-geology",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Engineering geology applies rock and soil properties to construction. Strength, discontinuities (RQD), weathering, permeability and clay behaviour (Atterberg limits) decide where dams, tunnels, foundations, cuttings and quarries are safe; slopes are judged by a factor of safety.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the geotechnical properties of rocks and soils and calculate RQD and the plasticity index",
        "Explain the geological requirements of a dam site and its reservoir",
        "Explain how dip, faults, water and rock type affect tunnels, foundations and cuttings",
        "Calculate a factor of safety for a slope and state factors that influence the siting of a quarry",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Geotechnical property", "Uniaxial compressive strength", "Discontinuity", "RQD", "Atterberg limits", "Plasticity index", "Factor of safety", "Bearing capacity"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Cameroon's large dams, among them Edéa and Song Loulou on the Sanaga, Lagdo on the Benue and Lom Pangar in the East, store water for electricity and irrigation. Each was built only after geologists tested the rock beneath it. Roads in the western highlands cross steep weathered slopes where landslides follow heavy rain. **Engineering geology** is the use of geology to make such works safe and economical."),
        H("Geotechnical properties"),
        KEY("Properties that engineers measure", "Rock is judged as a material and as a mass.", cards=[
            ["Strength", "**Uniaxial compressive strength** (UCS): the stress at which a cylinder of rock fails under compression. Fresh granite and basalt are very strong; shale and weathered rock are weak."],
            ["Discontinuities", "Joints, bedding planes, faults and cleavage. Their spacing, orientation, openness and infill usually control the strength of a rock **mass** more than the strength of the rock itself."],
            ["Weathering grade", "From fresh rock (grade I) to residual soil (grade VI). Tropical weathering can reach tens of metres deep, so foundations may need to go deep."],
            ["Permeability and porosity", "Control leakage under dams, water inflow into tunnels and pore-water pressure in slopes."],
            ["Clay mineralogy", "Swelling clays (montmorillonite/smectite) expand when wet and shrink when dry, cracking buildings and roads."],
        ]),
        D("**Rock Quality Designation (RQD)** is the percentage of a drill-core run made up of intact pieces 10 cm or longer. Above 90% is excellent, 75 to 90% good, 50 to 75% fair, 25 to 50% poor and below 25% very poor.", title="RQD", formula=r"\text{RQD} = \frac{\sum \text{lengths of pieces} \ge 10 \text{ cm}}{\text{total length of core run}} \times 100\%"),
        EX("Worked example 1: RQD", intro="A 150 cm core run contains pieces of 25, 12, 8, 30, 5, 18 and 40 cm, plus small fragments. Calculate the RQD and classify the rock.", steps=[
            S("Pieces 10 cm or longer", math=r"25 + 12 + 30 + 18 + 40 = 125 \text{ cm}"),
            S("RQD", math=r"\frac{125}{150} \times 100 = 83\%"),
            S("Classify", text="Between 75 and 90%: good quality rock. The 8 cm and 5 cm pieces do not count."),
        ]),
        D("The **Atterberg limits** describe how a clay soil behaves with water. The **liquid limit** (LL) is the water content at which the soil starts to flow like a liquid; the **plastic limit** (PL) is the water content below which it stops being plastic and crumbles. The **plasticity index** PI = LL − PL. A high PI means a clay that changes volume and strength greatly with water.", title="Atterberg limits"),
        EX("Worked example 2: plasticity index", intro="A clay has LL = 62% and PL = 28%. Calculate the PI and comment on its use as a foundation.", steps=[
            S("PI", math=r"62 - 28 = 34"),
            S("Comment", text="A PI above about 30 indicates a highly plastic clay, probably with swelling minerals: likely to heave and shrink, a poor foundation without treatment."),
        ]),
        H("Dams and reservoirs"),
        KEY("Requirements of a dam site", "The dam and its reservoir must both be safe.", cards=[
            ["Strong, impermeable foundation", "Fresh igneous or metamorphic rock or massive sandstone at shallow depth; no thick weathered zone or soft clay."],
            ["No active faults or shear zones", "They leak, weaken the foundation and may move in earthquakes."],
            ["Favourable dip", "Beds dipping **upstream** resist sliding and leakage; beds dipping **downstream** can slide and carry water under the dam."],
            ["Watertight reservoir", "No limestone with caves, no permeable beds or faults leading out of the basin; stable valley sides that will not slide when wetted."],
            ["Materials nearby", "Clay for the core, sand, gravel and rock for fill and concrete."],
        ]),
        IMG("algyu2-damdip.svg", alt="Two cross-sections of a dam across a valley on dipping beds. Left: beds dip upstream beneath the reservoir, labelled favourable. Right: beds dip downstream under the dam, with arrows showing leakage and possible sliding along bedding, labelled unfavourable",
            caption="Figure 1. The effect of dip on a dam site (schematic)."),
        H("Tunnels, foundations and cuttings"),
        T("A **tunnel** is easiest in uniform, strong, unjointed rock above the water table. Tunnels driven **along the strike** stay in the same bed; tunnels **across the strike** pass through many beds with different properties. Fault zones, weak shales and water-bearing sandstones cause collapses and flooding and need support and drainage. **Foundations** must rest on material with enough **bearing capacity**, the load per unit area the ground can carry without failure or excessive **settlement**; deep weathering or swelling clay may require piles. **Cuttings** for roads are stable when discontinuities dip into the slope, and unstable when they dip out of the slope towards the road (**daylighting**)."),
        H("Slope stability"),
        D("The **factor of safety** (FS) of a slope is the ratio of the forces resisting sliding to the forces driving sliding. FS > 1 is stable; FS < 1 fails; engineers usually design for about 1.3 to 1.5. For a dry, cohesionless slope, FS = tan φ / tan β, where φ is the angle of internal friction and β the slope angle.", title="Factor of safety", formula=r"\text{FS} = \frac{\text{resisting forces}}{\text{driving forces}}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: is the slope stable?", intro="A dry sand slope of 25° has an angle of internal friction of 35°. Calculate the factor of safety. What happens if heavy rain reduces the effective friction angle to 22°?", steps=[
            S("Dry slope", math=r"\text{FS} = \frac{\tan 35^\circ}{\tan 25^\circ} = \frac{0.700}{0.466} = 1.50"),
            S("Wet slope", math=r"\text{FS} = \frac{\tan 22^\circ}{\tan 25^\circ} = \frac{0.404}{0.466} = 0.87"),
            S("Interpret", text="Dry, the slope is stable (FS = 1.5). Water (pore pressure) lowers the resistance and FS drops below 1: failure. This is why landslides follow heavy rain."),
        ]),
        H("Quarries"),
        T("A quarry for aggregate or building stone is sited where the rock is of suitable quality (strength, durability, low clay content), in a large enough volume with little **overburden**, close to markets and roads, with drainage, and at a safe distance from homes, water supplies and unstable slopes. Environmental effects (dust, noise, blasting vibration, scarring) and land rehabilitation must also be planned."),
        MISTAKE("Listing effects of quarrying when asked for factors of siting", "The Board's examiners noted candidates who wrote about the disadvantages or effects of quarries when the question asked for factors that influence where a quarry is located. Read the command and give geological, economic and environmental factors of location."),
        MISTAKE("Testing only the rock, not the rock mass", "A strong granite core can still give a weak rock mass if it is closely jointed or faulted. Always discuss discontinuities and RQD."),
        QUIZ("For a dam, which orientation of the beds is most favourable?", ["Beds dipping steeply downstream", "Beds dipping upstream", "Beds cut by a fault parallel to the valley", "Beds of cavernous limestone"], 1,
             "Upstream dips resist sliding and reduce leakage under the dam.", hint="Which way should water have to flow to escape?",
             feedback=["Downstream dips allow sliding and seepage under the dam.", "", "A fault is a leakage path and a weakness.", "Caves in limestone allow reservoir leakage."]),
        QUIZ("A core run of 200 cm has intact pieces of 10 cm or longer adding up to 90 cm. What is the RQD?", ["90%", "55%", "45%", "22%"], 2,
             "90 ÷ 200 × 100 = 45%: poor rock.", hint="Divide by the total length.",
             feedback=["90 is the length in cm, not the percentage.", "That is 100 − 45.", "", "That is 45 ÷ 2."]),
        TIP("Engineering geology appears in Paper 2 (dam sites, tunnels, quarries, landslides) and in Paper 1 calculations (RQD, PI, FS). Always tie each requirement to a geological reason.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe the geological factors that should be investigated before building a dam and its reservoir.",
           "Foundation rock: strength, depth of weathering, permeability; discontinuities and RQD; faults and seismic risk; dip of beds (upstream favourable); soluble rocks such as limestone; reservoir watertightness (permeable beds, faults leading out of the basin); stability of valley sides when saturated; sediment supply that could silt the reservoir; availability of construction materials. Methods: mapping, boreholes, core logging, permeability tests, geophysics."),
        PQ("A clay has a liquid limit of 48% and a plastic limit of 22%. A road cut in sand has slope 30° and friction angle 32°. Calculate the PI and the dry factor of safety, and comment.",
           "PI = 48 − 22 = 26: a medium-to-high plasticity clay, some volume change. FS = tan 32° ÷ tan 30° = 0.625 ÷ 0.577 = 1.08: just above 1, so marginally stable when dry and likely to fail when wet; it should be flattened or drained."),
        PQ("State five factors that influence the location of a quarry.",
           "Quality of the rock (strength, durability, low clay or reactive silica content); quantity and accessibility of rock with thin overburden; distance to markets and access by road or rail; drainage and water table; distance from settlements, water supplies and protected areas (dust, noise, blasting); land ownership and permits; potential for rehabilitation after closure."),
        SUM("Rock masses are judged by strength, discontinuities, weathering, permeability and clay mineralogy.",
            "RQD = sum of core pieces ≥ 10 cm ÷ run length × 100; PI = LL − PL.",
            "Dam sites need strong impermeable foundations, no faults, upstream dips and a watertight reservoir.",
            "Tunnels, foundations and cuttings depend on dip, faults, water and weathering; FS = resisting ÷ driving forces.",
            "Quarries are sited on good rock with thin overburden, near markets, away from homes and water supplies."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 16. Examination: Paper 1
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_EXAM,
    topic="Paper 1",
    title="Paper 1: multiple-choice strategy for A Level Geology",
    slug="gyu2-exam-paper-1-multiple-choice",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Paper 1 has 50 multiple-choice questions over the whole syllabus (30% of the examination, per the Board's 2023 report). This lesson gives a routine for reading, predicting, eliminating and checking, and practises the calculation, diagram and fossil-identification questions typical of geology.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the format and weighting of Paper 1 as given in the Board's 2023 report",
        "Apply a four-step routine to multiple-choice questions, including NOT and EXCEPT stems",
        "Answer calculation and diagram-based items quickly and accurately",
        "Plan revision across both years of the course",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Stem", "Distractor", "Elimination", "Negative stem", "Calculation item", "Time management"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Paper 1 rewards wide, accurate knowledge. In 2023 it had 50 multiple-choice questions covering the entire syllabus, both Lower and Upper Sixth, each worth one mark and marked electronically; it carried 30% of the examination (Board's 2023 report). The report shows that most questions test knowledge and comprehension, with a smaller share of application, analysis and evaluation. So the basics must be secure, and the harder items need a method."),
        D("The **stem** is the question or incomplete statement. The **key** is the correct option. **Distractors** are the wrong options, written to attract candidates who have made a common mistake.", title="Parts of a multiple-choice item"),
        H("A four-step routine"),
        KEY("Read, predict, eliminate, check", "Use it on every item.", cards=[
            ["1. Read the stem", "Underline the command and any negative word (NOT, EXCEPT, LEAST). Note units and what is being asked: a rock, a mineral, a process, an age?"],
            ["2. Predict", "Before looking at the options, think of the answer. A prediction protects you from persuasive distractors."],
            ["3. Eliminate", "Cross out options that are wrong for a definite reason. Two options that say the same thing are usually both wrong."],
            ["4. Check", "Re-read the stem with your choice. In a NOT question, make sure you chose the false statement."],
        ]),
        H("Typical geology items"),
        KEY("Item types and tactics", "Recognize the type, then use its tactic.", cards=[
            ["Definition and recognition", "Fossil groups, minerals, rock types, structures, units. Learn exact definitions: the distractor is often a near-miss (a cast for a mould, a period for a system)."],
            ["Calculation", "Half-lives, Darcy flow, RQD, concentration factors, true dip, grain-size statistics. Work it out on the question paper; distractors are often unfinished or inverted calculations."],
            ["Diagram", "Range charts, cross-sections, phase diagrams, suture lines, maps. Read the axes and the key first."],
            ["Order of events", "Cross-cutting, superposition and unconformities. Write the sequence in the margin, oldest at the bottom."],
            ["Application to Cameroon", "Volcanic Line, basins, craton, resources. Choose the option that matches the well-established fact, not a guessed detail."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: a calculation item", intro="A mineral contains one quarter of its original parent isotope. The half-life is 1 250 million years. What is its age? A 312.5 Ma  B 1 250 Ma  C 2 500 Ma  D 5 000 Ma", steps=[
            S("Predict", text="One quarter left means two half-lives."),
            S("Calculate", math=r"2 \times 1\,250 = 2\,500 \text{ Ma}"),
            S("Check distractors", text="A is a quarter of the half-life; B is one half-life; D is four half-lives. The key is C."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a NOT item", intro="Which of the following is NOT a feature of irregular echinoids? A bilateral symmetry  B periproct outside the apical system  C Aristotle's lantern in all forms  D petaloid ambulacra", steps=[
            S("Underline NOT", text="We want the false statement."),
            S("Test each", text="A, B and D are true for irregular echinoids. Most irregular echinoids lack a lantern, so C is false."),
            S("Answer", text="C."),
        ]),
        H("Timing and revision"),
        T("The Board does not publish a time for each question. In KKEVO's mock (a practice setting) you have 90 minutes for 50 items, about 1.8 minutes each. Do a first pass answering everything you are sure of, mark the hard ones and return to them. Never leave a blank: the report describes one mark per question and mentions no penalty for wrong answers, so a guess after elimination is better than nothing."),
        T("Spread revision over all ten content modules of both years. The 2023 report advises candidates to avoid speculative preparation (studying only some topics) and to learn correct spellings of technical terms: in Paper 1 a misread term (bytownite and labradorite, anticline and antiform) leads straight to a distractor."),
        MISTAKE("Answering from the options instead of the stem", "Reading the options first lets a familiar word pull you in. Predict first, then look."),
        MISTAKE("Missing the negative word", "In a NOT or EXCEPT stem three options are true. Circle the negative word before you start."),
        QUIZ("According to the Board's 2023 report, what does Geology Paper 1 consist of?", ["50 multiple-choice questions, 30%", "6 essay questions, 35%", "5 compulsory practical questions, 25%", "40 multiple-choice questions, 40%"], 0,
             "Paper 1 is 50 multiple-choice questions weighted 30%.", hint="It is the multiple-choice paper.",
             feedback=["", "That is Paper 2.", "That is Paper 3.", "Both the number and the weighting are wrong."]),
        QUIZ("A range chart question asks for the beds in which two species occur together. This is a:", ["Definition item", "Diagram item testing a concurrent-range zone", "Calculation item", "Order-of-events item"], 1,
             "You read the overlap of two ranges on a diagram.", hint="Which zone is the overlap of two ranges?",
             feedback=["It needs the diagram, not just a definition.", "", "No calculation is needed.", "It is about co-occurrence, not sequence."]),
        TIP("Paper 1 tests all modules of both years. Revise with short, mixed sets of questions (like the KKEVO practice sets) and check every wrong answer against the lesson.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Explain why predicting the answer before reading the options helps in a multiple-choice paper.",
           "Distractors are designed to look right to a candidate with a common misconception. If you have already worked out the answer from the stem, you can recognize the key and are less likely to be attracted by a familiar but wrong option. It also saves time, because you are checking rather than comparing four statements."),
        PQ("Water flows through an aquifer of K = 8 m per day with a gradient of 0.01 across an area of 100 m². Options: A 0.8  B 8  C 80  D 800 m³ per day. Choose and explain the distractors.",
           "Q = KAi = 8 × 100 × 0.01 = 8 m³ per day: B. A is 8 × 0.01 × 10, a slip in the area; C uses a gradient of 0.1 instead of 0.01; D leaves out the gradient (8 × 100). Each distractor is a typical slip."),
        PQ("Draw up a revision plan for Paper 1 that covers both years of the course in the last six weeks before the examination.",
           "Week 1: geophysics and plate tectonics, crystallography. Week 2: optical and systematic mineralogy, igneous petrology. Week 3: metamorphic petrology, sedimentology. Week 4: structural geology and map work, palaeontology. Week 5: stratigraphy and Cameroon, economic geology. Week 6: mixed timed sets of 50 questions, review of errors. Each week: learn definitions, redo calculations, and answer 20 to 30 mixed MCQs."),
        SUM("Paper 1: 50 multiple-choice questions over the whole syllabus, 30% (Board's 2023 report).",
            "Routine: read the stem, predict, eliminate, check; circle NOT and EXCEPT.",
            "Calculation distractors are unfinished or inverted calculations: always work it out.",
            "Diagram items: read axes, keys and scales first.",
            "Revise every module; never leave a blank; KKEVO's 90 minutes is a practice setting."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 17. Examination: Paper 2
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_EXAM,
    topic="Paper 2",
    title="Paper 2: planning and writing geology essays",
    slug="gyu2-exam-paper-2-essays",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Paper 2 is the essay paper: in 2023 it had six questions, of which candidates answered four (35% of the examination). This lesson covers choosing questions, command words, planning, labelled diagrams and the errors the Board's examiners reported.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the structure of Paper 2 from the Board's 2023 report",
        "Interpret command words and choose questions you can answer fully",
        "Plan an essay with points, examples, diagrams and evaluation",
        "Avoid the errors reported by the Board's examiners",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Command word", "Essay plan", "Labelled diagram", "Evaluation", "Relevance", "Technical vocabulary"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In 2023 Paper 2 had six questions, of which candidates answered any four; it carried 35% of the examination (Board's 2023 report). The questions had several parts testing knowledge, comprehension, application, analysis, synthesis and evaluation. Performance was weak, and the report explains why: many candidates wrote what they knew about a topic instead of what the question asked."),
        H("Choosing your four questions"),
        T("Read all six questions before you start. For each, check every part: a question that looks easy may end with a part you cannot do. The report lists topics that many schools treat weakly, among them metamorphism, crystallography, stratigraphy, palaeontology and map work, and warns against speculative preparation that skips parts of the syllabus. Choose the four where you can answer every part."),
        H("Command words"),
        KEY("What each command asks", "Answer the command, not the topic.", cards=[
            ["Define / state", "A precise statement or fact, no explanation."],
            ["Describe", "Say what something is like or what happens, in order, with detail."],
            ["Explain / account for", "Give reasons and mechanisms: how and why."],
            ["Distinguish / compare", "Point-by-point differences (and similarities), ideally in a table."],
            ["Discuss / evaluate / assess", "Arguments for and against, strengths and limits, and a reasoned conclusion."],
        ]),
        H("Planning an answer"),
        KEY("A 5-minute plan", "Write it on the answer sheet before you start.", cards=[
            ["Key terms", "Define the main terms in the question."],
            ["Points", "List 5 to 8 points in a logical order (time, process, scale)."],
            ["Examples", "Add a named example or Cameroonian case for each main point."],
            ["Diagram", "Decide which labelled diagram will carry several points."],
            ["Evaluation", "For discuss or evaluate questions, note the limits and your conclusion."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: a plan for an explanation", intro="Question: 'Explain the formation of oil and gas and describe two types of trap.'", steps=[
            S("Terms", text="Petroleum, source rock, kerogen, reservoir, seal, trap."),
            S("Formation", text="Organic matter in anoxic fine sediment; burial; kerogen; oil window about 60 to 120 °C; gas window above; primary and secondary migration by buoyancy."),
            S("Traps", text="Anticline (diagram: seal, gas, oil, water) and stratigraphic pinch-out (diagram). One labelled diagram each."),
            S("Finish", text="State the timing condition: the trap must form before migration."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a plan for a discussion", intro="Question: 'Discuss the hypotheses for the origin of the Cameroon Volcanic Line.'", steps=[
            S("Describe the line briefly", text="Extent, trend, ocean-continent setting, alkaline rocks."),
            S("Hypotheses with evidence", text="Hotspot (for: alkaline basalts; against: no age progression). Hot line. Lithospheric weakness along Pan-African shear zones. Edge-driven convection at the craton margin."),
            S("Conclusion", text="A combination of mantle upwelling and lithospheric control best fits the evidence; the question remains open."),
        ]),
        H("What the examiners reported"),
        T("The 2023 report gives many lessons. Candidates confused a lava flow with a sill instead of using evidence such as baked and chilled margins, vesicles and xenoliths. They drew the rock cycle instead of Bowen's reaction series. They described folds when asked for the major structures associated with folds. They explained why fossils are preserved without saying whether the preservation potential was high or low. They described the theory of sea-floor spreading instead of explaining how the evidence supports it. They wrote effects of quarrying when asked for factors of location. In every case they answered a question that was not set."),
        MISTAKE("Writing everything you know about the topic", "Underline the command word and the exact focus. A good, short, relevant answer beats a long one that misses the point."),
        MISTAKE("Unlabelled or decorative diagrams", "A diagram earns credit only when it is clear, large and labelled with the features you discuss. Refer to it in the text."),
        MISTAKE("Abbreviations and misspellings", "The report asks candidates not to use abbreviations and short-hand, and notes misspelt mineral names. Write terms in full and spell them correctly: plagioclase, bytownite, anticline, dip."),
        QUIZ("A question asks: 'Explain how palaeomagnetic stripes support sea-floor spreading.' Which answer is best?", ["A description of the theory of sea-floor spreading", "Symmetrical stripes of normal and reversed magnetization on both sides of the ridge, getting older away from it, show that new crust formed at the ridge and moved outwards", "A list of reversals of the Earth's field", "A description of continental drift evidence"], 1,
             "It links the evidence to the conclusion, which is what explain asks.", hint="The question asks how the evidence supports the theory.",
             feedback=["This describes the theory, not the evidence.", "", "A list of reversals does not show spreading.", "That is a different body of evidence."]),
        QUIZ("Which command word requires arguments for and against and a conclusion?", ["State", "Describe", "Evaluate", "Define"], 2,
             "Evaluate (or discuss, assess) asks for a judgement.", hint="Which word asks for a judgement?",
             feedback=["State needs a short fact.", "Describe needs what, not why or how good.", "", "Define needs a precise meaning."]),
        TIP("Use your planning time. A plan of points, examples and one or two labelled diagrams for each of four questions is the best protection against irrelevance.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Plan an answer to: 'Distinguish between a lava flow and a sill.'",
           "Use a table. Lava flow: cools at the surface, upper surface irregular, vesicular or scoriaceous and possibly weathered; baked rocks only below; overlying sediments may contain fragments of the flow; fine-grained to glassy; often basalt. Sill: intruded between beds below the surface; upper and lower contacts both chilled, with baked rocks above and below; xenoliths from above and below; rarely vesicular; medium-grained; often dolerite."),
        PQ("Plan an answer to: 'Describe Bowen's reaction series and explain its significance.'",
           "Diagram with two branches: discontinuous (olivine, pyroxene, amphibole, biotite) and continuous (plagioclase from calcic anorthite through bytownite, labradorite, andesine, oligoclase to sodic albite), joining at low temperature in K-feldspar, muscovite and quartz. Explain: order of crystallization from basaltic magma on cooling; reaction of early crystals with melt; fractional crystallization changes the melt composition. Significance: explains associations of minerals, the order of weathering resistance (Goldich) and magmatic differentiation."),
        PQ("Explain why evaluation and a conclusion matter in a 'discuss' question.",
           "Discuss asks for a balanced judgement. Giving only one side, or a list without weighing the evidence, answers 'describe' instead. Examiners reward arguments for and against, recognition of limits and uncertainty, and a conclusion that follows from the evidence."),
        SUM("Paper 2 in 2023: six questions, answer any four, 35% (Board's 2023 report).",
            "Read every part of all six questions before choosing.",
            "Obey the command word: define, describe, explain, distinguish, evaluate.",
            "Plan points, examples, labelled diagrams and evaluation.",
            "Avoid the reported errors: answering a different question, unlabelled diagrams, abbreviations and misspellings."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 18. Examination: Paper 3
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_EXAM,
    topic="Paper 3",
    title="Paper 3: practical skills with maps, crystals, fossils, photographs and specimens",
    slug="gyu2-exam-paper-3-practical",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Paper 3 is the practical paper of five compulsory questions (25%), with school-based assessment and fieldwork (5% each). It tests geological maps and sections, crystal models, fossils, photographs of structures and hand specimens. This lesson gives a method for each, using the Board's comments.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the structure of Paper 3 as given in the Board's 2023 report",
        "Locate and describe folds, faults, outliers and inliers on a geological map and draw a section",
        "Describe crystals, fossils and hand specimens systematically and give evidence for each identification",
        "Interpret photographs of structures and relationships between specimens",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Grid reference", "Dip arrow", "Outlier", "Inlier", "Cross-section", "Crystal axes", "Mode of life", "Hand specimen description"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Paper 3 is a practical paper of five compulsory questions, weighted 25%, with school-based assessment and fieldwork weighted 5% each (Board's 2023 report). Most of its marks test application and analysis: you must look at a map, a model, a fossil, a photograph or a rock and say what it is and why. A systematic method gives marks even when an identification is wrong."),
        H("Geological maps"),
        KEY("Describing a structure on a map", "Follow the order.", cards=[
            ["Locate", "Give a **six-figure grid reference**, eastings first, then northings."],
            ["Identify", "Anticline or syncline (on a map of beds, not antiform or synform unless the ages are unknown)."],
            ["Evidence", "For an anticline, the **dip arrows point away** from the axis and the oldest bed is in the core; for a syncline, they point towards the axis and the youngest is in the core."],
            ["Describe", "Trend of the axis written in full (north to south, or N-S), plunge if any, symmetry from the dips, core bed named."],
        ]),
        T("Faults: locate them, give the trend from one direction to another, the downthrow side (younger beds on the downthrown side after erosion), and the type from offsets. An **outlier** is an outcrop of younger rock completely surrounded by older rock, isolated by erosion; an **inlier** is older rock surrounded by younger rock. Both form by erosion, often of folded beds."),
        EX("Worked example 1: an outlier", intro="On a map, a small patch of shale (the youngest bed) at grid reference 432178 on an invented map is completely surrounded by older limestone. Identify the feature and account for its formation.", steps=[
            S("Identify", text="An outlier."),
            S("Evidence", text="A limited outcrop of the younger shale is completely surrounded by the older limestone."),
            S("Formation", text="The shale once covered a wider area; erosion (often helped by folding into a syncline) removed it except for this isolated patch."),
        ]),
        T("Sections: draw the topographic profile along the line, mark where each boundary crosses it, project the beds down with the correct dips, show fold axes and faults, and label beds as in the map key. Do not redraw the map."),
        H("Crystal models"),
        T("Insert the crystallographic axes with the positive ends labelled **+a, +b, +c** (not a+, b+). Draw the axes as they lie in the model: in a model with oblique axes, such as a triclinic crystal, they are inclined, not forced horizontal and at right angles. List the symmetry elements present; for the triclinic system write 'no plane, no axis, a centre of symmetry', not 'none'. Name the system with the diagnostic reason and give a mineral example."),
        H("Fossils"),
        KEY("Describing a fossil specimen", "Four headings.", cards=[
            ["Identification with reasons", "Group (and genus if possible) with the features you see: symmetry, sutures, pallial sinus, siphonal canal."],
            ["Age range", "The range of the group or genus, for example Cambrian to Permian for trilobites."],
            ["Mode of life with evidence", "For example: burrower (siphonal canal or pallial sinus); attached (pedicle foramen, cemented valve); swimmer (thin flat valves)."],
            ["Environmental significance", "For example: reef corals indicate a **warm, shallow, clear** marine environment; write all the adjectives."],
        ]),
        H("Photographs and hand specimens"),
        T("For a photograph, name the structure, describe it (shape, size, regularity) and explain its formation. For example, columnar joints are closely packed, mostly five- or six-sided vertical columns formed by contraction of cooling lava, which pulls the rock apart towards many centres."),
        KEY("Describing a hand specimen", "Description before identification.", cards=[
            ["Igneous", "Grain size, colour index (leucocratic, mesocratic, melanocratic), minerals, texture (porphyritic, vesicular, flow banding)."],
            ["Sedimentary", "Composition, grain size, shape, sorting, maturity, cement, structures, fossils, reaction with dilute HCl. Write 'coarse grained', not 'coarse grain'."],
            ["Metamorphic", "Texture (slaty, schistose, gneissose, granoblastic), minerals and porphyroblasts, foliation, parent rock and grade."],
            ["Minerals", "Colour, streak, lustre, hardness, cleavage, specific gravity, habit, reaction with acid."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: relationships between specimens", intro="Specimen E is a coarse-grained, pink rock of quartz, feldspar and mica. Specimen F is fine-grained and light-coloured with quartz phenocrysts and flow banding. State the relationship between E and F.", steps=[
            S("Identify", text="E is granite; F is rhyolite."),
            S("Composition", text="Both are acid igneous rocks with the same mineralogy (quartz, feldspar, mica)."),
            S("Relationship", text="E is the plutonic equivalent of F (F is the volcanic equivalent of E): same magma type, different cooling rate."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Four-figure grid references and northings first", "The Board's examiners noted four-figure references and northings given first. Use six figures: eastings, then northings."),
        MISTAKE("'Beds moving away' as evidence of an anticline", "The evidence is that the dip arrows point away from the axis and the oldest bed is in the core. Spell 'dip' correctly, not 'deep'."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing burrower with borrower", "A burrower digs into sediment. Spelling changes the meaning; examiners noted this error."),
        QUIZ("Which is the correct way to label the positive end of the vertical crystallographic axis?", ["c+", "+c", "C+", "z"], 1,
             "The convention is +a, +b, +c.", hint="The sign comes first.",
             feedback=["The examiners rejected this order.", "", "Axes are labelled with lower-case letters.", "Crystal axes are a, b and c."]),
        QUIZ("A small outcrop of an older rock is completely surrounded by younger rocks. What is it?", ["An outlier", "An inlier", "An angular unconformity", "A dyke"], 1,
             "Older rock surrounded by younger is an inlier.", hint="Older inside, younger around.",
             feedback=["An outlier is younger rock surrounded by older.", "", "An unconformity is a surface, not an isolated outcrop.", "A dyke is an intrusion that cuts across beds."]),
        TIP("In Paper 3, every identification needs evidence. Practise with real specimens, maps and models in school; the report advises regular practical work after each theory topic and the use of geological photographs.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe the steps for describing a fold on a geological map.",
           "Locate it with a six-figure grid reference (eastings first). Identify it as an anticline or syncline. Give the evidence: dip arrows pointing away from (anticline) or towards (syncline) the axis, oldest or youngest bed in the core. Give the trend of the axis in full (for example north to south), the plunge if the outcrops close, and the symmetry from the dip values. Name the core bed."),
        PQ("A hand specimen reacts with dilute HCl, is granular and sugary, and is composed of calcite. Identify it and give its mode of formation and its relationship to limestone.",
           "Marble. It forms by metamorphism (for example contact metamorphism) of a pure limestone, in which calcite recrystallizes into an interlocking granular (saccharoidal) texture. Limestone is its parent rock (protolith): both are made of calcite and react with dilute HCl."),
        PQ("Explain the formation of columnar joints and say what they indicate about the rock.",
           "As a lava flow or shallow intrusion cools, it contracts. Tensional stress acts towards many evenly spaced centres, opening joints that join into a network of mostly six-sided (or five-sided) columns perpendicular to the cooling surfaces. They indicate an igneous rock (usually basalt or dolerite) that cooled slowly and evenly from a surface."),
        SUM("Paper 3: five compulsory practical questions (25%), plus school-based assessment and fieldwork (5% each).",
            "Maps: six-figure grid references (eastings first), dip-arrow evidence, trends in full, outliers and inliers by erosion, sections from the profile.",
            "Crystals: label +a, +b, +c, list symmetry elements correctly, name the system with its reason.",
            "Fossils: identification with features, age range, mode of life with evidence, full environmental significance.",
            "Specimens: describe before identifying; state relationships such as plutonic and volcanic equivalents or protolith and metamorphic product."),
    ],
))
